  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-1a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a labelled sketch of a flexible rubber engine mount, showing the rubber element between the engine bracket and the frame bracket.

1. Draw the pad mount exactly as in 24-1a: engine bracket, steel plates, rubber pad, frame bracket, bolt and rebound washer.
2. Optionally add a V-mount: two inclined rubber blocks with an interleaf plate, meeting under the engine bracket, so load is shared between compression and shear.
3. Label rubber, steel plate, interleaf plate, engine bracket, frame bracket and the compression and shear arrows.

**Answer:** Rubber mount sketch with rubber bonded between steel plates, engine bracket on top and frame bracket below, showing compression and shear.

**Exam tip:** Draw the pad mount with all labels; add a small V-mount sketch if time allows. Slip: leaving out the load arrows.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-1b'), 1, '**What it asks:** Find how many g of sideways acceleration the car feels in the turn: convert speed to m/s, use $a = v^2/R$, then divide by $g$.

**Given:**
- $V = 60\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $R = 40\ \mathrm m$

**To find:**
- lateral acceleration $a_y$ in $g$

1. Convert speed.
$$v=\frac{V}{3.6}=\frac{60}{3.6}=16.67\ \mathrm{m/s}$$
2. Lateral acceleration.
$$a_y=\frac{v^2}{R}=\frac{16.67^2}{40}=6.94\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$$
3. In g.
$$\frac{a_y}{g}=\frac{6.94}{9.81}=0.71\,g$$

**Answer:** $a_y = 6.94\ \mathrm{m/s^2} = 0.71g$.

**Exam tip:** Convert km/h to m/s first, then $v^2/R$, then divide by 9.81 to state it in g. Slip: using km/h in the formula.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-1c'), 1, '**What it asks:** Find how often per second the wheel meets a bump on a road with 10 m wavelength, at three speeds (30, 60, 80 km/h). Convert km/h to m/s and compute $f = v/\lambda$ for each.

**Given:**
- $\lambda = 10\ \mathrm m$
- $V_1 = 30\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $V_2 = 60\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $V_3 = 80\ \mathrm{km/h}$

**To find:**
- (i) vertical frequency input $f$ at $30\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- (ii) $f$ at $60\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- (iii) $f$ at $80\ \mathrm{km/h}$

1. Convert speeds.
$$v=\frac{V}{3.6}:\ \frac{30}{3.6}=8.333,\ \frac{60}{3.6}=16.67,\ \frac{80}{3.6}=22.22\ \mathrm{m/s}$$
2. (i), (ii), (iii) Frequencies.
$$f=\frac{v}{\lambda}=\frac{v}{10}=0.833,\ 1.667,\ 2.222\ \mathrm{Hz}$$
3. (i) Comment: 30 km/h sits near the 1 Hz body bounce; 60 and 80 km/h are above it.

**Answer:** (i) $f = 0.833\ \mathrm{Hz}$ at $30\ \mathrm{km/h}$; (ii) $f = 1.667\ \mathrm{Hz}$ at $60\ \mathrm{km/h}$; (iii) $f = 2.222\ \mathrm{Hz}$ at $80\ \mathrm{km/h}$.

**Exam tip:** Show km/h to m/s, then $f = v/\lambda$ for each speed, and add a one-line comment about the 1 Hz body-bounce region. Slip: forgetting the comment when speeds sit near 1 Hz.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-1d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants two things: a list of every unit mounted on a chassis, and a ladder-frame sketch showing where each one sits.

1. List: engine mounts (two front, one rear), gearbox mount, radiator mount, body or cab mounts, spring hangers and shackles, shock absorber brackets, steering gear box mount, fuel tank brackets, battery tray, exhaust hangers, spare wheel carrier, bumper brackets.
2. Draw the ladder frame as in the 2024 Q1(d) steps below and mark each item at its location, front group, middle group, rear group.
3. Use a numbered key matching the list.
4. Also asked as 2024 Q1(d) (7 marks): schematically represent a ladder frame chassis and the various vehicular mountings on it. Write:
5. Draw the ladder frame: two side members, 4 to 5 cross members, plan view, front at the left.
6. Front end: radiator mount, two front engine mounts, steering gear box bracket, front spring hangers and shackles, front shock absorber brackets, front bumper brackets.
7. Middle: rear engine (gearbox) mount, body or cab mounts along both side members, fuel tank brackets, battery tray, exhaust hangers.
8. Rear end: rear spring hangers and shackles, rear shock absorber brackets, spare wheel carrier, rear bumper brackets.
9. Label each with a numbered key; use different marks for engine group, suspension group and body group.
10. Also asked as 2023 Q1(a) (2 marks): sketch a vehicle chassis. Write:
11. Draw two long side members (rails) as parallel lines in plan view.
12. Join them with 4 to 5 cross members so the shape is a ladder.
13. Front end: engine on the frame, with flywheel and clutch behind it, then the gearbox.
14. Behind the gearbox draw the propeller shaft running back to the differential on the rear axle.
15. Draw the front axle with steering at the front and the rear axle with wheels; label side member, cross member, engine, clutch, gearbox, propeller shaft, differential, front axle, rear axle.

**Answer:** Written list of the chassis mountings plus a ladder-frame plan view with each one located (placement detail in the 2024 Q1(d) steps below).

**Exam tip:** Give the written list first, then the numbered sketch matching it. Slip: sketching without the list or listing without locating.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-1e'), 1, '**What it asks:** Find the front and rear axle loads when standing (static) and while braking at 0.3g (dynamic), then the load transfer on a 1 in 10 climb. Work in consistent units: 3 tons to N, mm to m.

**Given:**
- $m = 3\ \text{tons} = 3000\ \mathrm{kg}$
- $l = 2400\ \mathrm{mm}$
- $a = 1300\ \mathrm{mm}$ (CG from front wheel)
- $h = 325\ \mathrm{mm}$
- braking deceleration $= 0.3g$
- gradient $1$ in $10$

**To find:**
- (i) static axle loads
- (ii) dynamic axle loads when braking at $0.3g$
- (iii) load transfer when climbing a gradient of 1 in 10

1. Data.
$$W=mg=3000\times9.81=29430\ \mathrm N,\ b=l-a,\ l=2.4,\ a=1.3,\ b=1.1,\ h=0.325\ \mathrm m$$
2. (i) Static loads.
$$R_f=\frac{Wb}{l}=13489\ \mathrm N,\quad R_r=\frac{Wa}{l}=15941\ \mathrm N$$
3. (ii) Braking at $0.3g$: transfer.
$$\Delta W=\frac{W(0.3)h}{l}=\frac{29430\times0.3\times0.325}{2.4}=1196\ \mathrm N$$
4. (ii) Dynamic loads.
$$R_f''=R_f+\Delta W=13489+1196=14684\ \mathrm N,\quad R_r''=R_r-\Delta W=15941-1196=14746\ \mathrm N$$
5. (iii) Gradient 1 in 10.
$$\tan\theta=0.1:\ \cos\theta=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+\tan^2\theta}}=0.9950,\ \sin\theta=\cos\theta\tan\theta=0.0995$$
6. (iii) Axle loads on the slope.
$$R_f=\frac{W(b\cos\theta-h\sin\theta)}{l}=13025\ \mathrm N,\quad R_r=\frac{W(a\cos\theta+h\sin\theta)}{l}=16259\ \mathrm N$$
7. (iii) Load transfer on the slope.
$$\frac{Wh\sin\theta}{l}=397\ \mathrm N;\quad \text{front change against static}=13489-13025=464\ \mathrm N\ \text{(to the rear)}$$
8. (iii) Check.
$$R_f+R_r=W\cos\theta:\ 13025+16259=29284=W\cos\theta$$

**Answer:** (i) Static: $R_f = 13489\ \mathrm N$, $R_r = 15941\ \mathrm N$. (ii) Braking at $0.3g$: $\Delta W = 1196\ \mathrm N$ moves to the front, $R_f = 14684\ \mathrm N$, $R_r = 14746\ \mathrm N$. (iii) Climbing 1 in 10: weight transfer $W h\sin\theta/l = 397\ \mathrm N$ from front to rear; $R_f = 13025\ \mathrm N$, $R_r = 16259\ \mathrm N$ (the front is $464\ \mathrm N$ below static because $W\cos\theta$ is also smaller).

**Exam tip:** State static loads, transfer term and final loads for each case, then check that front plus rear equals the weight. Slip: using the wrong sign when climbing.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-2a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a labelled sketch of the hinge that lets a front wheel steer: the axle beam end (fork), the knuckle between its jaws, the kingpin through both, the stub axle carrying the wheel, and the steering arm. Draw the front view; naming the type (Elliot) earns extra credit.

1. Draw the end of the axle beam as a yoke (fork) with two jaws, upper and lower.
2. Place the stub axle with its knuckle eye between the jaws and pass the kingpin vertically through yoke and knuckle, with bushes in the eye and a thrust bearing between the upper jaw and the top of the eye (the axle load presses down through it onto the stub axle).
3. Bolt the steering arm to the knuckle; draw the stub axle projecting outward carrying the wheel hub.
4. Mark the kingpin axis as the steering axis, and label axle beam, yoke, kingpin, stub axle, thrust bearing, steering arm, wheel hub.
5. Name the type under the sketch: Elliot (fork on the axle beam). Add one line: in the reversed Elliot the fork is on the stub axle and fits over a plain axle-beam end; in the Lemoine type an L-shaped stub axle is pinned below a plain axle-beam end (no fork); the Marmon type is a variant of Lemoine.

**Answer:** Axle-beam yoke, stub axle eye and kingpin form the knuckle joint; the kingpin axis is the steering axis about which the wheel pivots.

**Exam tip:** Draw a sectional front view with yoke, kingpin, bush, thrust bearing, stub axle and steering arm, all labelled. The usual slip is not showing the thrust bearing or the steering arm.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-2b'), 1, '**What it asks:** Find the number of steering-wheel turns lock to lock for steering ratios 25 and 50. Turns = ratio $\times$ road-wheel sweep $/ 360$; the paper omits the sweep, so assume $80^\circ$ lock to lock and say so.

**Given:**
- $i_1 = 25$
- $i_2 = 50$
- $\theta_s = 80^\circ$ (assumed road-wheel sweep, lock to lock)

**To find:**
- (i) turns of steering wheel $N$ for $i = 25$
- (ii) turns of steering wheel $N$ for $i = 50$

1. Relation:
$$N=\frac{i\,\theta_s}{360}$$
2. This is the reverse of 2024 Q2(b). There: turns given, find ratio, $i = 360N/\theta_s$. Here: ratio given, find turns, $N = i\,\theta_s/360$. Same relation, solved the other way.
3. Same assumption: road-wheel sweep $80^\circ$ lock to lock.
4. (i) For $i = 25$:
$$N=\frac{i\,\theta_s}{360}=\frac{25\times80}{360}=5.56\ \text{turns}$$
5. (ii) For $i = 50$:
$$N=\frac{i\,\theta_s}{360}=\frac{50\times80}{360}=11.11\ \text{turns}$$
6. From straight ahead to one lock the turns are half: $2.78$ and $5.56$.
7. If the paper means the steering gearbox ratio (steering wheel to drop arm), use the drop-arm sweep instead, about $70^\circ$ lock to lock (assumed):
$$N=\frac{i\,\theta_s}{360}:\ N_{25}=\frac{25\times70}{360}=4.86,\quad N_{50}=\frac{50\times70}{360}=9.72$$

**Answer:** (i) $N = 5.56$ turns lock to lock for $i = 25$ ; (ii) $N = 11.11$ turns lock to lock for $i = 50$ ($2.78$ and $5.56$ turns from centre to one lock). If $25$ and $50$ are taken as gearbox ratios with a $70^\circ$ drop-arm sweep: $4.86$ and $9.72$ turns. State the sweep you assume.

**Exam tip:** State the assumed sweep, show the formula, substitute and give turns lock to lock and one lock. Mention the other reading if the ratio is the gearbox ratio. The usual slip is silently assuming a sweep.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-2c'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a diagram of castor, camber and king pin inclination, and then a short explanation of two distances: the lateral and longitudinal ''reaction distances'' (scrub radius type offsets where the tyre force acts relative to the king pin axis).

1. Front view: wheel plane, steering axis, camber and KPI as drawn in the 2024 Q2(a) steps below. Extend the steering axis to the ground and mark the sideways distance from that point to the tyre contact centre: lateral reaction distance (scrub radius).
2. Side view: steering axis and castor angle. Mark the fore-aft distance between the steering-axis ground point and the contact centre: longitudinal reaction distance (castor trail).
3. State: lateral reaction distance is the lever arm of braking or tractive force about the steering axis; longitudinal reaction distance is the lever arm of side force.
4. Also asked as 2023 Q2(a) (2 marks): schematically represent king pin inclination and castor angle. Write:
5. Front view: draw the wheel as a rectangle on the ground line, the axle end and a steering axis (kingpin axis) through the wheel centre region, tilted so the top leans inward. Mark the angle between this axis and the vertical as kingpin inclination (KPI).
6. Side view: draw the wheel circle with hub centre, and the steering axis tilted so the top leans backward. Mark the angle to the vertical as castor, and mark the point where the axis meets the ground ahead of the contact patch (castor trail).
7. Label: steering axis, vertical, KPI, castor, direction of travel arrow, contact patch.
8. Also asked as 2024 Q2(a) (2 marks): schematically represent castor, camber and king pin inclination. Write:
9. Redraw the KPI and castor views exactly as in the 2023 Q2(a) steps above.
10. In the front view add the wheel centre plane and mark camber as its tilt from the vertical; positive when the top leans outward.
11. Label the three angles on their own view: camber and KPI in front view, castor in side view.

**Answer:** Lateral reaction distance = scrub radius (front view, sideways); longitudinal reaction distance = castor trail (side view, fore and aft). Some texts name each distance by the force it carries, which swaps the two labels; write both: scrub radius (sideways, lever arm of braking/drive force) and castor trail (fore-aft, lever arm of side force).

**Exam tip:** Redraw the camber, KPI and castor views, extend the steering axis to the ground and mark the two distances; add one line on what force each lever arm belongs to. Note both names in case the paper''s labels differ; a missing ground intercept is the usual slip.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-2d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants two sketches: a labelled layout of the steering system, and a diagram of the Ackerman principle (inner wheel turns more than outer so all axes meet at one point on the rear axle line).

1. Layout: as drawn in the 2023 Q2(d) steps below: wheel, column, gearbox, drop arm, drag link, steering arms, track rod, stub axles.
2. Ackermann sketch: draw the rear axle line extended sideways. Draw both front wheels turned and extend their axes (perpendicular to each wheel plane); they meet the extended rear axle line at one point O, the common centre.
3. Mark inner angle $\delta_i$ larger than outer angle $\delta_o$, wheelbase $L$ and the distance between kingpin centres $w$ (slightly less than the track).
4. Write the condition ($w$ = distance between the two kingpin (pivot) centres (m); $L$ = wheelbase (m); $\delta_i$, $\delta_o$ = inner and outer wheel steer angles):
$$\cot\delta_o-\cot\delta_i=\frac{w}{L}$$
5. State that the steering arms angled inward with the track rod form a trapezium that approximates this condition.
6. Also asked as 2023 Q2(d) (7 marks): schematically represent the various linkages used in a four-wheeler steering system. Write:
7. Draw the plan view of the front axle: axle beam with a kingpin and stub axle at each end, a steering arm on each stub axle angled slightly inward.
8. Join the two steering arms with the track rod (tie rod). With the track rod behind the axle the arms angle inward (their lines, extended, meet near the centre of the rear axle), so the track rod is shorter than the kingpin spacing and forms the Ackermann trapezium. If the track rod is ahead of the axle the arms must angle outward instead.
9. Draw the chain: steering wheel, steering column, steering gearbox, drop arm (Pitman arm) swinging in a plane, drag link to the steering arm of one stub axle.
10. Mark ball joints at the ends of the drag link and track rod, and the kingpins at the axle ends.
11. Third linkage, for independent front suspension: the single track rod is split. The drop arm moves a centre (relay) link supported at its other end by an idler arm pivoted on the frame, and two short tie rods with ball joints run from the centre link to the steering arms, so each wheel can rise and fall without steering the other.
12. Second small sketch: rack and pinion, pinion on the column, rack across the car, tie rods with ball joints to the steering arms on the knuckles.
13. Label every part.
14. Also asked as 2024 Q2(d) (7 marks): schematically represent the steering system layout in a four wheeler. Write:
15. Draw the chain exactly as in the 2023 Q2(d) steps above on a plan view of the car.
16. Show the front axle, rear axle and wheel base outline so the layout reads as a four wheeler.
17. Add the rack and pinion version in one corner if time remains.
18. Label all parts.

**Answer:** Layout as listed, plus Ackermann: all wheel axes meet at one centre on the rear axle line, $\cot\delta_o-\cot\delta_i=w/L$.

**Exam tip:** Draw the layout first, then a separate Ackermann sketch with the rear axle extended and the common centre marked; write the equation $\cot\delta_o - \cot\delta_i = w/L$. The usual slip is swapping inner and outer angle.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-2e'), 1, '**What it asks:** In a turn the car body leans outward (body roll). Find how many degrees it leans. The springs resist the lean: their stiffness is given per degree of lean, front and rear. The body is pushed by the sideways force at its CG, acting with a lever arm of 200 mm about the roll axis.

**Given:**
- $m = 2000\ \mathrm{kg}$
- $K_f = 1750\ \mathrm{Nm/deg}$
- $K_r = 380\ \mathrm{Nm/deg}$
- $a_y = 0.25\ g$ (lateral acceleration)
- $h = 200\ \mathrm{mm} = 0.2\ \mathrm{m}$ (CG to roll axis)
- $H_{cg} = 0.5\ \mathrm{m}$ (CG height above ground, not needed)

**To find:**
- roll angle $\phi$ in degrees

1. Step 1. Turn ''0.25 g'' into m/s². Here $k = 0.25$.
$$a_y = k\,g = 0.25\times9.81 = 2.4525\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$$
2. Step 2. Sideways force on the body, acting outward at the CG.
$$F = m\,a_y = 2000\times2.4525 = 4905\ \mathrm{N}$$
3. Step 3. This force twists the body about the roll axis. Lever arm = CG to roll axis = 200 mm = 0.2 m. (The 0.5 m CG height above the ground is NOT used: the body leans about the roll axis, not about the ground.)
$$M = F\,h = 4905\times0.2 = 981\ \mathrm{N\,m}$$
4. Step 4. Front and rear springs both resist the lean, so their stiffnesses add.
$$K = K_f + K_r = 1750 + 380 = 2130\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}$$
5. Step 5. The body leans until spring torque = roll moment.
$$\phi = \frac{M}{K} = \frac{981}{2130} = 0.461^\circ$$
6. Extra (if asked how the moment splits): each end takes moment in proportion to its stiffness. Front $M_f = M K_f/K = 981\times1750/2130 = 806\ \mathrm{N\,m}$; rear $M_r = 981\times380/2130 = 175\ \mathrm{N\,m}$. The stiffer front carries most of it.
7. Refinement (only if you have time): as the body leans, the CG shifts sideways and the weight adds a moment $m g h \phi$. With $K$ in N m/rad: $K = 2130\times180/\pi = 122040\ \mathrm{N\,m/rad}$, $m g h = 2000\times9.81\times0.2 = 3924\ \mathrm{N\,m/rad}$:
$$\phi=\frac{m\,a_y\,h}{K-m\,g\,h}=\frac{981}{122040-3924}=0.00831\ \mathrm{rad}=0.476^\circ$$

**Answer:** Roll angle $\phi = M/K = 981/2130 \approx 0.46^\circ$ (body leans outward).

**Exam tip:** Write each step with units: lateral force, moment about roll axis, total stiffness, angle in degrees. Marks go for using $0.2\ \mathrm{m}$ as the arm and for adding $K_f$ and $K_r$; the usual slip is using the CG height $0.5\ \mathrm{m}$.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-3a'), 1, '**What it asks:** Same as the earlier tyre question: draw a labelled tyre cross-section and state what aspect ratio means (height divided by width, as a percentage).

1. Sketch as in 23-3a: tread, belt, carcass, sidewall, bead, inner liner, rim, with $H$ and $W$ marked.
2. State the definition.
$$\text{aspect ratio}=\frac{H}{W}\times100\,\%$$
3. Quote one example such as 245/75, where $H=183.75\ \mathrm{mm}$.

**Answer:** Aspect ratio $= (H/W)\times100\,\%$ (section height over section width); labelled cross-section as in 23-3a.

**Exam tip:** Same answer as 23-3a: neat labelled cross-section plus the aspect-ratio formula with a number example. Common slip: unlabelled drawing, or width and height swapped.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-3b'), 1, '**What it asks:** Same as 23-3c: decode 245/75 R16 and work out the tyre radii from width, aspect ratio and rim size.

**Given:**
- $W = 245\ \mathrm{mm}$ (section width)
- $AR = 75\,\%$ (aspect ratio)
- $D_{in} = 16\ \mathrm{in}$ (rim diameter)

**To find:**
- (i) free outer radius $r_{out}$
- (ii) static loaded radius $r_s$
- (iii) dynamic rolling radius $r_d$

1. Decode: width 245 mm, aspect 75 %, radial, rim 16 in.
2. Rim diameter and section height.
$$D_r=D_{in}\times25.4=16\times25.4=406.4\ \mathrm{mm},\quad H=AR\times W=0.75\times245=183.75\ \mathrm{mm}$$
3. (i) Outer diameter and radius.
$$D=D_r+2H=406.4+2(183.75)=773.9\ \mathrm{mm},\quad r_{out}=\frac{D}{2}=386.95\ \mathrm{mm}$$
4. (ii) Static loaded radius, with a typical ratio (assumed, not given): $k_s \approx 0.96$ of the free radius.
$$r_s\approx k_s\,r_{out}=0.96\times386.95=371.5\ \mathrm{mm}$$
5. (iii) Dynamic rolling radius, with a typical ratio (assumed, not given): $k_d \approx 0.98$ of the free radius.
$$r_d\approx k_d\,r_{out}=0.98\times386.95=379.2\ \mathrm{mm}$$

**Answer:** (i) Free outer radius $r_{out} = 386.95\ \mathrm{mm}$, from rim radius $203.2\ \mathrm{mm}$, section height $183.75\ \mathrm{mm}$; (ii) static loaded radius $r_s \approx 371.5\ \mathrm{mm}$ (assumed $0.96$); (iii) dynamic rolling radius $r_d \approx 379.2\ \mathrm{mm}$ (assumed $0.98$).

**Exam tip:** Write the code breakdown first, convert inches to mm, then show each radius on its own line with units. Common slip: forgetting to double the sidewall height for the diameter, or mixing inches and mm.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-3c'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a sketch of the tyre contact patch with arrows showing the vertical, lateral and longitudinal forces acting on it while rolling.

1. Draw the contact patch in plan view as a rounded rectangle with the direction of travel marked.
2. Vertical force: shade the vertical pressure over the patch (side view: a hump, skewed toward the leading edge when rolling); its resultant $F_z$ acts a distance $e$ ahead of the wheel centre, giving the rolling resistance moment $F_z e$.
3. Longitudinal force: tangential traction arrows along the direction of travel; driving pushes the tyre forward, braking rearward; resultant $F_x$. In free rolling the shear is S-shaped (one way at the front, the other at the rear) and nets to $F_x\approx0$.
4. Lateral force: draw sideways traction arrows over the patch, building up toward the rear, so the resultant $F_y$ acts behind the patch centre; that distance is the pneumatic trail $t_p$.
5. State the shared limit.
$$\sqrt{F_x^2+F_y^2}\le \mu F_z$$

**Answer:** Plan-view patch showing vertical pressure, longitudinal traction and lateral traction distributions with resultants $F_z$, $F_x$, $F_y$; combined limit $\sqrt{F_x^2+F_y^2}\le \mu F_z$.

**Exam tip:** Draw a plan view of the patch with arrows for vertical, longitudinal and lateral force, labelled; add three short lines of explanation. Common slip: drawing only the vertical pressure.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-3d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants tread pattern sketches for five uses: steered wheel, rear (drive) wheel of a commercial vehicle, off-roader, earthmover and winter tyre, and why each differs.

1. General rule: circumferential ribs give lateral (side) grip and guidance; transverse bars and lugs give longitudinal (traction and braking) grip; grooves drain water; sipes add biting edges.
2. Steered wheel: rib tread, 3 to 5 circumferential (straight or zig-zag) grooves running round the tyre, with drainage grooves; ribs resist side slip and give lateral guidance, precise steering and low noise.
3. Rear (drive) wheel of a commercial vehicle: strong transverse or diagonal bars and blocks (lug or rib-lug tread, cross grooves at the shoulders) with drainage gaps, which transmit driving and braking force.
4. Off-roader: bold, widely separated blocks with open grooves for grip on loose ground while still usable on tarmac.
5. Earthmover: very strong, widely spaced deep blocks (lugs) that resist cutting and chipping on rough quarry ground.
6. Winter tyre: many blocks cut by fine sipes (biting edges), wide grooves to clear snow and slush, and a softer compound that stays flexible in the cold.
7. Label ribs, grooves, blocks, lugs and sipes, and give a one-line duty beside each sketch.
8. Also asked as 2023 Q3(e) OR (7 marks): tread profile for commercial vehicle, off-roader, tractor and winter tyres. Write:
9. Commercial vehicle, off-roader and winter tread as in the steps above; the extra item is the tractor tread.
10. Tractor: diagonal or herringbone bars (lugs) with open gaps between them for soil penetration and self-cleaning in soft ground.
11. Also asked as 2023 Q3(a) (2 marks): what is aspect ratio for a tyre, show the cross section of a tyre. Write:
12. Draw the transverse section as an inverted U around the rim: thick tread at the top, bracing belt (crown plies) just below it, carcass plies running down both sidewalls and wrapping round the beads.
13. Draw the two steel-wire beads seated on the rim flanges, the inner liner (tubeless) lining the inside, and the enclosed air space.
14. Mark the dimensions: section width $W$ (across the sidewalls), section height $H$ (bead seat to tread crown), rim diameter $D_r$.
15. Aspect ratio is section height divided by section width, as a percentage.
$$\text{aspect ratio}=\frac{H}{W}\times100\,\%$$
16. Example: in 245/75, $H = 0.75\times245 = 183.75\ \mathrm{mm}$. A lower aspect ratio means a shorter sidewall relative to the width.
17. Also asked as 2024 Q3(d) (7 marks): tyre cross section and tyre tread patterns for off road, earthmoving and commercial vehicle. Write:
18. Cross-section: draw and label as in the 2023 Q3(a) steps above (tread, belt, carcass, sidewall, bead, liner, rim, with $H$ and $W$). Then the off-road, earthmoving and commercial treads exactly as in the tread steps above, with one line of duty each.
19. Also asked as 2025 Q3(a) (2 marks): represent a tyre cross section and state what aspect ratio is. Write:
20. The labelled cross-section of the 2023 Q3(a) steps above, plus the definition below with one example.
$$\text{aspect ratio}=\frac{H}{W}\times100\,\%,\quad 245/75:\ H=0.75\times245=183.75\ \mathrm{mm}$$

**Answer:** Five labelled tread sketches with duty lines: steered wheel, commercial rear wheel, off-roader, earthmover, winter.

**Exam tip:** Five small labelled sketches with a one-line duty each; about one page. Common slip: forgetting the steered-wheel rib pattern.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-3e'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the construction (a sketch plus short description) of drum brakes for four layouts: leading-trailing, both leading, both trailing, and duo servo.

1. Draw the drum with its rotation arrow, backplate, shoes, linings, springs and wheel cylinder as in the 2024 Q3(e) steps below.
2. Leading-trailing: one wheel cylinder, a fixed anchor, one self-energising and one de-energising shoe.
3. Both leading: two single-acting cylinders, one at the toe of each shoe, each shoe on its own anchor; drum rotates from cylinder to anchor on both shoes, so both self-energise in forward rotation (both trail in reverse).
4. Both trailing: the same two-cylinder, two-anchor layout reversed, so both friction moments oppose application; more pedal effort (servo) but a stable brake, little affected by fade.
5. Duo-servo: the lower shoe ends are joined by a floating adjuster link, with a single anchor pin at the top and a double-acting wheel cylinder just below it. The primary shoe is dragged round and pushes the secondary through the link, so the primary''s self-energising force is fed into the secondary (servo action); it works the same in forward and reverse.
6. Compare (brake factor for the same input): duo-servo highest, then two-leading, then leading-trailing, two-trailing lowest; higher self-energising means less pedal effort but more sensitivity to lining friction change (less stable).
7. Also asked as 2024 Q3(e) OR (7 marks): represent the mounting of a drum brake system for both leading, both trailing and leading-trailing. Write:
8. Draw the drum with a rotation arrow first (forward direction), the backplate, two shoes with linings, the return springs and the actuator.
9. (a) Both leading (two-leading): two single-acting wheel cylinders, one at the toe of each shoe, and each shoe pivots on its own anchor at the opposite end; the drum rotates from each cylinder towards that shoe''s anchor, so in forward rotation both shoes self-energise. In reverse both become trailing.
10. (b) Both trailing (two-trailing): the same two-cylinder, two-anchor layout turned the other way, so the drum rotates from each shoe''s anchor towards its cylinder; both friction moments oppose application, so more input force (usually a servo) is needed, but the brake is stable and less sensitive to lining friction change and fade.
11. (c) Leading-trailing: one double-acting wheel cylinder between the upper shoe tips and a common fixed anchor between the lower ends; one shoe leads and one trails, the roles swap in reverse, so braking is the same both ways (suits rear wheels with the handbrake).
12. Mark the anchor, wheel cylinder, shoes, linings, springs, drum rotation and the friction direction on each shoe.

**Answer:** Four labelled drum arrangements with rotation arrows; duo-servo has a floating adjuster link, one upper anchor and servo action from primary to secondary shoe.

**Exam tip:** Four neat layouts with rotation arrows, anchors and cylinders; add a note on which is most self-energising. Common slip: leaving out the floating link in the duo-servo.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-4a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the exhaust pollutants of an engine listed, each with a short note on how it forms.

1. Write CO, HC, $\mathrm{NO_x}$, PM and $\mathrm{CO_2}$ with the cause of each exactly as in the 2023 Q4(b) steps below.
2. Add that CO and HC come from incomplete combustion, $\mathrm{NO_x}$ from high combustion temperature, and PM mainly from diesel combustion.
3. Also asked as 2023 Q4(b) (2 marks): list the tail pipe emissions in an automobile. Write:
4. Tail-pipe emissions are what leaves the exhaust pipe after combustion. List them with their cause.
5. Carbon monoxide (CO): incomplete combustion, rich mixture.
6. Unburned hydrocarbons (HC) and other organic gases: fuel that did not burn (misfire, quench layers, rich mixture).
7. Oxides of nitrogen ($\mathrm{NO_x}$): at high combustion temperature, $\mathrm{N_2}$ and $\mathrm{O_2}$ from the air combine. $\mathrm{NO_x}$ peaks near stoichiometric to slightly lean (about $5$ to $10$ percent excess air), where temperature is highest and spare oxygen is present. Rich and very lean mixtures give less.
8. Particulate matter (PM, soot): mainly diesel engines and gasoline direct injection.
9. Carbon dioxide ($\mathrm{CO_2}$): complete combustion product; not toxic but a greenhouse gas. Water vapour and nitrogen are the bulk harmless products.
10. Sulphur dioxide ($\mathrm{SO_2}$) from sulphur in fuel can be added as a minor item. Ozone is not a direct exhaust product (it forms in the atmosphere); fuel-tank evaporation and crankcase blow-by are vehicle emissions but not tail-pipe ones.

**Answer:** CO, unburned HC, $\mathrm{NO_x}$, particulate matter, $\mathrm{CO_2}$.

**Exam tip:** List CO, HC, $\mathrm{NO_x}$, PM and $\mathrm{CO_2}$ with the cause of each in one line; for 2 marks five names with a short cause is enough. Common slip: adding ozone or fuel-tank vapour, which are not tail-pipe emissions.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-4b'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a sketch showing where the starter motor sits next to the engine flywheel and how its pinion meshes with the flywheel ring gear.

1. Draw the crankshaft centre and the flywheel as a large circle with ring-gear teeth on its rim.
2. Draw the starter motor beside the engine block or bell housing with its axis parallel to the crankshaft, a small pinion at its nose meshing the ring gear.
3. Draw the starter bolted to the engine/bell housing flange; the nose bearing locates the pinion at the correct mesh depth.
4. Add the solenoid (magnetic switch) on top, the shift lever and a double arrow showing pinion travel.
5. Label: crankshaft, flywheel, ring gear, pinion, overrunning (one-way) clutch, starter motor, solenoid, shift lever, engine block/bell housing, battery lead.
6. Operation note: solenoid pushes the pinion into mesh and closes the heavy contacts; the motor cranks; overrunning clutch lets the pinion free-wheel when the engine fires; the pinion then retracts.

**Answer:** Starter mounted on the engine or bell housing with its pinion meshing the flywheel ring gear, engaged by a solenoid and shift lever.

**Exam tip:** Draw flywheel with ring gear, pinion, motor, solenoid and shift lever in one neat side view and label at least 8 parts; common slip is leaving out the overrunning clutch or the solenoid, which is what makes the pinion engage.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-4c'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a plain list of the indicators and gauges found on a dashboard. Name them and say what each shows.

1. Write the grouped list from the 2023 Q4(a) steps below (safety group, operation group).
2. Add the one-line function against every name; 3 marks reward breadth plus a short purpose.
3. Example of the function lines to write: speedometer = road speed; tachometer = engine rpm; fuel gauge = tank level; turn signal = shows the intended turn to other drivers; high beam = main headlamp is on, dip before oncoming traffic.
4. Also asked as 2023 Q4(a) (2 marks): list the indicators used on the dashboard of a vehicle. Write:
5. Start with the definition: a warning lamp reports a fault or unsafe condition, an indicator reports a normal operating state.
6. Safety and warning group: oil pressure lamp, coolant temperature (overheat) lamp, battery/charging lamp, brake warning lamp (low fluid or parking brake applied), ABS lamp, seat belt lamp, door-ajar lamp, airbag lamp, engine malfunction (check engine) lamp.
7. Operation group: fuel level gauge and low-fuel lamp, speedometer and odometer, tachometer, direction indicator (turn signal) arrows, hazard flasher, high-beam lamp, parking/headlamp-on lamp.
8. One-line function for each (needed for the 3-mark version): oil lamp = lubrication pressure low; temperature lamp = engine overheating; battery lamp = charging system fault, not a charge gauge; brake lamp = fluid low or parking brake on; ABS lamp = anti-lock system fault; belt lamp = seat belt not fastened; fuel lamp = low fuel; turn arrows = signal in use; high-beam lamp = main beam on (not a fault).
9. Many lamps glow for a moment at ignition-on as a bulb self-check; a lamp that stays on after the engine starts is the warning.
10. Also asked as 2024 Q4(c) (3 marks): list the dashboard indicators used for vehicle safety and operation. Write:
11. Keep the two group headings from the 2023 Q4(a) steps above and give each item a one-line function.
12. Vehicle safety: oil pressure, brake, ABS, seat belt, airbag, door ajar, engine overheating, battery/charging fault, engine malfunction (check engine).
13. Vehicle operation: fuel level, speedometer, tachometer, turn signals, hazard flasher, high-beam, headlamp-on.

**Answer:** Dashboard indicators: oil pressure, coolant temperature, battery, brake, ABS, seat belt, door ajar, airbag, engine malfunction, fuel, speedometer, tachometer, turn signal, hazard, high beam, each with its one-line function as in the 2023 Q4(a) steps below.

**Exam tip:** Make two short columns (safety/warning and operation) with 8 to 10 items and a 3 to 5 word function against each; the common slip is writing names only, or calling the battery lamp a charge gauge.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-4d'), 1, '**What it asks:** Two parts, no numbers: (1) draw and label an epicyclic (planetary) gear train and say how it is built; (2) explain how an automatic gearbox uses it, i.e. which member is held or driven to get each gear, and what holds them (clutches and brakes).

1. Construction (draw this): a SUN gear at the centre; 3 or 4 PLANET gears around it, each meshing with the sun and with the RING (annulus) gear that has internal teeth; the planets run on pins fixed to a PLANET CARRIER. Sun, carrier and ring all share one axis, and each has its own shaft or drum.
2. Label: sun gear, planet gears, planet pins, planet carrier, ring (annulus) gear, input shaft, output shaft, band brake, multi-plate clutch.
3. Working rule: of the three members, hold one, drive the second, take output from the third. Which member is held decides the gear:
4. Ring held, sun drives, carrier output: speed reduction (low gear), $i = 1 + Z_r/Z_s$.
5. Sun held, carrier drives, ring output: overdrive, output faster than input.
6. Carrier held, sun drives, ring output: reverse, $i = -Z_r/Z_s$.
7. Any two members locked together: the whole set turns as one, direct drive (ratio 1).
8. Use in automatic transmission: the gears stay in mesh all the time. Multi-plate CLUTCHES connect a member to the input, and BAND BRAKES or brake clutches hold a member still. The valve body sends oil pressure to the right clutch or brake, so the gear changes without the driver using a clutch pedal. Two or three planetary sets (Simpson or Ravigneaux) give 3 to 6 forward gears and reverse.
9. A torque converter in front of the gearbox replaces the friction clutch: it multiplies torque at low speed and lets the car stand still in gear; a lock-up clutch removes slip at cruising speed.
10. Advantages to write: compact (all gears coaxial), load shared by several planets, gears always in mesh so smooth shifts without power interruption, easy to automate.

**Answer:** Sketch the sun, planets on a carrier and the ring gear. Hold one member, drive another, take output from the third: ring held gives reduction, sun held gives overdrive, carrier held gives reverse, two locked gives direct drive. In an automatic gearbox, hydraulically applied clutches and band brakes choose which member is driven and which is held, so gears change without a clutch pedal.

**Exam tip:** 7 marks: half for a neat labelled sketch (sun, planets, carrier, ring, brake band, clutch), half for the hold/drive table and the clutch-and-brake explanation. No numbers are asked; do not invent tooth counts.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-4e'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a labelled sketch of a single plate dry clutch, showing the parts that clamp and release the friction disc.

1. Reproduce the answer of 2023 Q4(d) (steps below): draw the section in the same order (flywheel, cover, pressure plate and spring, driven plate with two linings on the splined hub, release bearing and fork, input shaft).
2. Add the torque-path arrows, the two-line working and the torque formula (no numbers are asked). Here z = number of friction surfaces, mu = coefficient of friction, W = total axial spring force on the plate (N), R_m = mean friction radius (m). For uniform wear (worn-in clutch, the usual design assumption) $R_m=\frac{r_1+r_2}{2}$. For uniform pressure (new clutch) $R_m=\frac{2}{3}\,\frac{r_1^3-r_2^3}{r_1^2-r_2^2}$, where $r_1$ and $r_2$ are the outer and inner lining radii.
$$T=z\,\mu\,W\,R_m$$
3. Also asked as 2023 Q4(d) (7 marks): show the schematic construction of a single plate dry type clutch. Write:
4. Purpose line: a friction clutch connects and disconnects the engine from the gearbox, allows smooth take-up from rest and a break in drive for gear changes.
5. Draw the engine crankshaft ending in the flywheel as a large vertical disc; the flywheel is bolted to the crank flange.
6. Bolt the clutch cover to the flywheel rim. Inside the cover draw the pressure plate facing the flywheel and the spring that loads it (the diaphragm spring in the lab drawing, or a ring of coil springs).
7. Between the flywheel and pressure plate draw the driven plate (clutch disc): a thin steel disc with a friction lining on each face (two faces, hence two friction surfaces), cushion segments between the linings, and a central hub fitted with torsional damper springs.
8. Splined hub: draw the hub sliding on the splined gearbox input (clutch) shaft, whose spigot end runs in a pilot bush in the crankshaft.
9. Release mechanism: release (throw-out) bearing on a sleeve over the input shaft acting on the diaphragm fingers or release levers, operated by the fork and the pedal linkage or hydraulic cylinder. In the lab drawing (pull type) the fingers are pulled to release.
10. Show the engaged torque path with arrows: engine, flywheel and cover, pressure plate, friction linings, driven plate hub, splined shaft to gearbox. Add a second small arrow for release motion.
11. Working in two lines: pedal released, spring clamps the disc between flywheel and pressure plate and torque is transmitted by friction; pedal pressed, the pressure plate is withdrawn, the clamping load goes and the engine turns without driving the gearbox.
12. Closing: torque capacity is limited by friction, $T = z\mu W R_m$, where z = number of friction surfaces, mu = coefficient of friction, W = total axial spring force on the plate (N), R_m = mean friction radius (m). For uniform wear (worn-in clutch, the usual design assumption) $R_m=\frac{r_1+r_2}{2}$. For uniform pressure (new clutch) $R_m=\frac{2}{3}\,\frac{r_1^3-r_2^3}{r_1^2-r_2^2}$, where $r_1$ and $r_2$ are the outer and inner lining radii.
$$T=z\,\mu\,W\,R_m,\quad z=2$$
13. Also asked as 2024 Q4(e) OR (7 marks): show the schematic construction of a single plate dry clutch (same wording). Write:
14. Same answer as the 2023 Q4(d) steps above.
15. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Functions and requirements of a clutch.** Connects and disconnects engine and gearbox for starting and gear change, transmits torque without slip when engaged, engages gradually for smooth take-off, dissipates heat well, and has a damper spring (torsional vibration damper) in the hub to absorb torque fluctuations.
16. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Friction lining materials.** Asbestos-free organic (resin bonded), semi-metallic, ceramic or Kevlar facings riveted or bonded to the disc; required: high and steady coefficient of friction ($\mu \approx 0.3$ to $0.4$), good heat resistance and wear resistance.
17. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Hydraulic clutch actuation.** Pedal moves a master cylinder, fluid pressure moves a slave cylinder that operates the release fork. Needs no linkage adjustment, gives smooth action and a flexible layout compared with a cable or rod linkage.

**Answer:** Single plate dry clutch: flywheel; driven plate with two friction linings, cushion segments and a damper-spring hub sliding on the splined input shaft; pressure plate and diaphragm (or coil) spring in a cover bolted to the flywheel; release bearing, fork and pedal linkage. The spring clamp transmits torque by friction, $T=z\mu W R_m$ with $z=2$ and $R_m=(r_1+r_2)/2$; pressing the pedal removes the clamp.

**Exam tip:** Draw a clean sectional half-view in the order flywheel, friction disc, pressure plate, spring, cover, release bearing and fork, input shaft, and label every part; add two lines on working. Common slip: one friction face instead of two, or missing the splined hub.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-5a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a definition of gear backlash (what it is) and then the method used to measure it. Two short parts: ''what'' and ''how measured''.

1. **Definition:** gear backlash is the free clearance between the non-driving flanks of meshing teeth, i.e. the amount one gear can be turned while the mating gear is held still.
2. **Measurement:** fix a dial indicator to the housing with its probe on a crown wheel tooth, at right angles to the tooth, in the measuring direction.
3. Hold the pinion stationary and rock the crown wheel gently through its free movement; the indicator travel is the backlash at that point.
4. Repeat at several positions around the crown wheel and compare with the service-manual limit.
5. Why it matters: too little backlash makes the teeth bind and wear and whine; too much gives noise and shock (clunk) when the torque direction reverses.

**Answer:** Backlash = free clearance between meshing tooth flanks, measured with a dial indicator on a crown wheel tooth while the pinion is held and the crown wheel is rocked.

**Exam tip:** Define in one line, then give 3 to 4 steps for dial-indicator measurement and the reason for repeating around the wheel. Slip: measuring with the pinion free to turn.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-5b'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the gearbox output shaft speed for two cases: 1st gear (ratio 3:1) and the Vth gear (Roman numeral V = 5th gear, ratio 0.8:1). Use $N_{out} = N_{engine}/\text{ratio}$ for each.

**Given:**
- $N_{engine} = 3500\ \mathrm{rpm}$
- $i_1 = 3:1$
- $i_5 = 0.8:1$

**To find:**
- (a) output shaft speed in first gear $N_{out,1}$
- (b) output shaft speed in fifth gear $N_{out,5}$

1. Ratio convention.
$$N_{out} = \frac{N_{engine}}{i}$$
2. (a) First gear, 3:1.
$$N_{out}=\frac{N_{engine}}{i}=\frac{3500}{3}=1166.7\ \mathrm{rpm}$$
3. (b) Fifth gear, 0.8:1 (the paper''s "Vth").
$$N_{out}=\frac{N_{engine}}{i}=\frac{3500}{0.8}=4375\ \mathrm{rpm}$$
4. (b) The fifth gear output turns faster than the engine, so it is an overdrive. These are gearbox output speeds, not wheel speeds (the final drive reduces them further).

**Answer:** (a) $N_{out,1} = 1166.7\ \mathrm{rpm}$; (b) $N_{out,5} = 4375\ \mathrm{rpm}$.

**Exam tip:** Write $N_{out} = N_{in}/i$, substitute with units and box the answers. Slip: multiplying by the ratio instead of dividing.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-5c'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the approximate acceleration of the car at 60 km/h: find tractive force $F = T i / r$ from engine torque (no resistance data is given, so resistances are ignored or assumed small), then $a = F/m$.

**Given:**
- $m = 2\ \mathrm{t}$
- $i = 4:1$
- $T = 247\ \mathrm{N\,m}$
- $V = 60\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $r = 375\ \mathrm{mm}$

**To find:**
- tentative acceleration $a$

1. Tractive force.
$$F = \frac{T\, i}{r} = \frac{247(4)}{0.375} = 2634.7\ \mathrm{N}$$
2. Acceleration with $m = 2000$ kg.
$$a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{2634.7}{2000} = 1.317\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$$
3. The speed of 60 km/h is not needed in this constant-torque force form; it would be needed only for power or drag.

**Answer:** $a = 1.32\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$ (tentative).

**Exam tip:** Write force then acceleration as two clean lines with units. Slip: using diameter in place of radius.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-5d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants two things at 1000, 2500 and 3500 rpm: (1) engine power $P = 2\pi N T/60$ with the given torque, and (2) the power the road demands, $P_{res} = (F_{roll}+F_{air}) v$, where speed $v$ comes from rpm, gear ratio and wheel radius. You must pick sensible $C_d$ and $C_{rr}$ yourself.

**Given:**
- $T = 247\ \mathrm{N\,m}$
- $N = 1000,\ 2500,\ 3500\ \mathrm{rpm}$
- $r = 325\ \mathrm{mm}$
- $i = 11:1$
- $m = 3000\ \mathrm{kg}$
- $A = 4\ \mathrm{m^2}$

**To find:**
- (i) engine power $P_e$ at 1000, 2500, 3500 rpm
- (ii) road resistance power $P_{road}$ at the same rpm

1. (i) Engine power at each speed.
$$P_e = \frac{2\pi N T}{60}$$
2. Road speed from rpm, overall ratio 11 and radius 0.325 m, no slip.
$$v = \frac{2\pi r N}{60\, i}$$
3. (ii) Road resistance with assumed values.
$$F_{road} = C_r m g + \tfrac12\rho C_d A v^2,\qquad C_r m g = 0.015(3000)(9.81) = 441.45\ \mathrm{N}$$
4. (ii) Road resistance power.
$$P_{road} = F_{road}\, v$$
5. At 1000 rpm: $P_e = 25.866$ kW; $v = 3.094$ m/s (11.14 km/h); $F_{road} = 450.8$ N; $P_{road} = 1.395$ kW.
6. At 2500 rpm: $P_e = 64.664$ kW; $v = 7.735$ m/s (27.85 km/h); $F_{road} = 500.1$ N; $P_{road} = 3.868$ kW.
7. At 3500 rpm: $P_e = 90.530$ kW; $v = 10.829$ m/s (38.98 km/h); $F_{road} = 556.4$ N; $P_{road} = 6.025$ kW.
8. Engine power rises linearly with rpm at constant torque, while road power rises faster (the drag term grows with $v^3$) but stays far below engine power, so surplus power is large at these low road speeds. Compare road power with $\eta P_e$ if losses are included.
9. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Engine power from torque and speed.** $P = \dfrac{2\pi N T}{60}$ (W) with $N$ in rpm and $T$ in N m; the power curve rises with speed, peaks, then falls as torque drops, while road-resistance power $P_R = R\,v$ rises roughly with $v^3$.
10. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Maximum speed and reserve power.** Maximum speed is where the engine power at the wheels $\eta P$ equals road-resistance power; the difference $\eta P - P_R$ at any speed is the reserve power available for acceleration or climbing a gradient.
11. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Resistances that make up road load.** Rolling $R_r = f\,W$, aerodynamic $R_a = \tfrac{1}{2}\rho\,C_d\,A\,v^2$ and gradient $R_g = W\sin\theta$; aerodynamic drag dominates at high speed and rolling resistance at low speed.

**Answer:** (i) engine power $P_e = 25.87,\ 64.66,\ 90.53\ \mathrm{kW}$; (ii) road resistance power $P_{road} = 1.39,\ 3.87,\ 6.02\ \mathrm{kW}$ at 1000, 2500, 3500 rpm respectively.

**Exam tip:** Tabulate rpm, engine power, speed, resistance and road power in one table. Slip: mixing rpm and rad/s or km/h and m/s.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '25-5e'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a neat labelled drawing of a differential (the gear set that lets the left and right wheels turn at different speeds), with every main part named.

1. Draw the casing outline and the propeller shaft entering from the left with the flange.
2. Draw the bevel pinion on the shaft meshing with the crown wheel (ring gear).
3. Draw the differential cage fixed to the crown wheel, supported on two bearings.
4. Inside the cage draw the cross-pin (spider) with two planet pinions and two side gears on either side, each splined to a half shaft.
5. Draw the half shafts leading to the left and right wheels. Label all parts.
6. One line of working: straight, cage and side gears turn together; in a turn the planets rotate and $n_L + n_R = 2 n_{cage}$.
7. Also asked as 2024 Q5(d) (7 marks): represent the assembly of the differential and say how proper tooth contact is achieved between crown wheel and pinion. Write:
8. Assembly and working line exactly as in the steps above. Then the tooth-contact method:
9. Tooth contact method: first set pinion depth and bearing preload with shims; set crown wheel position and backlash with the side adjusters; coat teeth with an approved marking compound; rotate under light braking in both directions and read the contact patch.
10. Read the pattern: pinion depth mainly moves the contact in tooth height (face/flank), crown wheel position moves it along the tooth length (heel/toe). Adjust until the patch is well spread, centred on the flank and away from the edges, then recheck backlash and preload.
11. Corrections: heavy face (high) contact, move the pinion in towards the crown wheel axis (deeper). Heavy flank (low) contact, move the pinion out. Heavy toe contact, move the crown wheel away from the pinion (this increases backlash). Heavy heel contact, move the crown wheel towards the pinion (this reduces backlash). After each move, recheck backlash (typically about 0.15 to 0.25 mm, see the service manual) and bearing preload.
12. Draw one tooth showing face, flank, heel and toe with the correct contact patch shaded. Exact pattern and limits come from the service manual.
13. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Differential action on a turn.** The outer wheel travels further than the inner, so the shafts turn at different speeds, with the speeds adding to twice the crown wheel speed: $N_L + N_R = 2N_c$. In a straight line the spider gears do not rotate; on a turn they rotate on their pins.
14. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Torque split and wheel-spin problem.** The plain differential gives equal torque to both wheels, so when one wheel loses grip the other receives no useful torque and the vehicle stalls. Remedies are the limited-slip differential, Torsen (worm gear) differential or differential lock.
15. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Differentials in four-wheel drive.** Front, centre and rear differentials are used, with the centre differential splitting torque between the front and rear axles; it can be locked or controlled by a viscous coupling for traction.

**Answer:** Labelled schematic: propeller shaft, bevel pinion, crown wheel, cage, cross-pin, planet pinions, side gears, half shafts, bearings, casing. Tooth contact (also asked 2024 Q5(d)): set by pinion depth shims and crown wheel adjusters, checked with marking compound and backlash.

**Exam tip:** Neat schematic with every part labelled and one line on the working formula. Slip: drawing the crown wheel on the wrong side of the pinion or leaving out the half shafts.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-1a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a labelled sketch of the rubber pad mount that sits between engine and frame, and one line on what the rubber does (it absorbs vibration).

1. Draw the frame cross member at the bottom with a flat bracket on it.
2. Place the rubber pad on it, bonded between two steel plates (top and bottom).
3. Put the engine bracket on the top plate and show the through-bolt with a rebound washer limiting upward movement.
4. Label engine bracket, steel plates, rubber pad, frame bracket, bolt, and arrows for compression (weight) and shear (torque reaction).

**Answer:** Rubber pad sandwiched between engine bracket and frame bracket, bonded to steel plates, with through-bolt and rebound washer.

**Exam tip:** Draw a neat cross-section sandwich and label bracket, plates, rubber, bolt, rebound washer and the two load arrows. Slip: drawing plain rubber with no steel plates.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-1b'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the formula that gives the natural frequency of a spring suspension using only the static deflection, i.e. start from $\omega_n = \sqrt{k/m}$ and replace $k/m$ by $g/\delta_{st}$.

1. At equilibrium the spring force balances the weight.
$$mg=k\delta\ \Rightarrow\ \frac{k}{m}=\frac{g}{\delta}$$
2. Substitute in the natural frequency.
$$f_n=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{k}{m}}=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{g}{\delta}}\ \mathrm{Hz}$$
3. With $\delta$ in metres this is about $0.5/\sqrt{\delta}\ \mathrm{Hz}$, or $30/\sqrt{\delta}$ cycles per minute (the form used in 2023 Q1(e)).

**Answer:** $f_n = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{g/\delta_{static}}$, approximately $0.5/\sqrt{\delta}\ \mathrm{Hz}$ for $\delta$ in metres.

**Exam tip:** Show $mg = kx$, substitute, and state $f = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{g/x}$, with the $30/\sqrt{x}$ cycles per minute form as a bonus. Slip: mixing mm and m.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-1c'), 1, '**What it asks:** Combine the spring and the tyre (two springs in series) into one ride rate, then use that and the load to get the natural frequency. Units are lb and in, so convert carefully.

**Given:**
- $k_s = 143\ \mathrm{lb/in}$
- $k_t = 1198\ \mathrm{lb/in}$
- $W = 957\ \mathrm{lb}$

**To find:**
- (i) ride rate $k_r$
- (ii) natural frequency $f_n$

1. (i) Ride rate for springs in series.
$$k_r=\frac{k_sk_t}{k_s+k_t}=\frac{143\times1198}{143+1198}=127.75\ \mathrm{lb/in}$$
2. (ii) Mass in imperial units from the $957\ \mathrm{lb}$ load, $g = 386\ \mathrm{in/s^2}$.
$$m=\frac{W}{g}=\frac{957}{386}=2.479\ \mathrm{lb\,s^2/in}$$
3. (ii) Natural frequency.
$$f_n=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{k_r}{m}}=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{127.75}{2.479}}=1.14\ \mathrm{Hz}$$
4. (ii) Static deflection cross-check.
$$\delta=\frac{957}{127.75}=7.49\ \mathrm{in},\quad f_n=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{386}{7.49}}=1.14\ \mathrm{Hz}$$

**Answer:** (i) $k_r = 127.75\ \mathrm{lb/in}$; (ii) $f_n = 1.14\ \mathrm{Hz}$ (about $68$ cycles/min), close to the comfortable $1\ \mathrm{Hz}$.

**Exam tip:** Write the series formula, then mass = W/g in imperial units, then f. Quote the answer next to the 1 Hz comfort figure. Slip: adding the two stiffnesses instead of using the series formula.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-1d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a sketch of a ladder-shaped frame (two side rails, cross-members) with arrows or labels showing what bolts onto it: engine, gearbox, radiator, fuel tank, body, axles and so on.

1. Draw the ladder frame: two side members, 4 to 5 cross members, plan view, front at the left.
2. Front end: radiator mount, two front engine mounts, steering gear box bracket, front spring hangers and shackles, front shock absorber brackets, front bumper brackets.
3. Middle: rear engine (gearbox) mount, body or cab mounts along both side members, fuel tank brackets, battery tray, exhaust hangers.
4. Rear end: rear spring hangers and shackles, rear shock absorber brackets, spare wheel carrier, rear bumper brackets.
5. Label each with a numbered key; use different marks for engine group, suspension group and body group.

**Answer:** Ladder frame with the standard mountings located: engine (3-point) and gearbox mounts, radiator, body mounts, spring hangers and shackles, shock absorber brackets, steering box, fuel tank, battery, exhaust hangers, spare wheel carrier, bumper brackets.

**Exam tip:** Draw the ladder plan view, then mark 8-10 mountings at their correct locations with a numbered key. Slip: dumping a list with no positions on the frame.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-1e'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a bullet list of the ways to keep noise and vibration out of the passenger area of a normal car, for example engine mounts, sound insulation, balanced engine, damped panels, good tyres.

1. Source measures: well-balanced engine, rubber engine and gearbox mounts, silencer on the exhaust with flexible hangers, balanced propeller shaft, low-noise tyre tread.
2. Path measures: rubber body and subframe mounts, stiff box and swaged body sections that raise panel frequencies, door and window seals.
3. Vibration measures in the chassis: shock absorbers and rubber suspension bushes, hydraulic (fluid-filled) engine mounts, balanced wheels and tyres, torsional vibration damper on the crankshaft, dual-mass flywheel.
4. Insulation (barrier): dense, heavy layers such as loaded PVC/heavy rubber mats and bitumen-backed felt on the floor, bulkhead (dash/firewall) and wheel arches to block sound passing through panels.
5. Damping: bitumen/mastic sound-deadening pads bonded to doors, side panels, floor and roof to stop panels ringing.
6. Absorption: polyurethane foam, mineral wool or bonded fibre on side panels, roof lining and engine compartment to soak up sound.
7. Glazing and seals: laminated or thicker acoustic glass, double door seals, sealed grommets where cables and pedals pass through the bulkhead.
8. Active measures (modern cars): active noise cancellation through the audio speakers, active engine mounts.

**Answer:** Control noise and vibration by source treatment (balance, mounts, silencer, tyres), path treatment (isolating mounts, suspension bushes and dampers, stiff body, seals, acoustic glass) and cabin treatment (insulation, damping, absorption materials at floor, bulkhead, doors, roof), plus active noise cancellation in modern cars.

**Exam tip:** Use three headings (source, path, cabin) with 2-3 measures each, and name the locations (floor, firewall, doors, roof). Slip: only mentioning the silencer.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-2a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a front view and side view of a wheel with three angles drawn and named: camber (wheel tilt from vertical), castor (king pin tilt seen from the side) and king pin inclination (king pin tilt seen from the front).

1. Redraw the KPI and castor views as in 2023 Q2(a).
2. In the front view add the wheel centre plane and mark camber as its tilt from the vertical; positive when the top leans outward.
3. Label the three angles on their own view: camber and KPI in front view, castor in side view.

**Answer:** Camber = tilt of the wheel plane from vertical (front view); KPI = inward tilt of the steering axis (front view); castor = backward tilt of the steering axis (side view).

**Exam tip:** Draw the front view with both the wheel plane and the steering axis, and the side view for castor; write the three angle names on their own views. Do not confuse camber (wheel lean) with KPI (axis lean); that is the most common lost mark.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-2b'), 1, '**What it asks:** Find the steering ratio (steering wheel angle divided by road wheel angle) for two cases. The wording is garbled: it means one steering wheel turns 3.5 turns lock to lock, and another turns 8 times, and you compare the two ratios.

**Given:**
- $N_1 = 3.5\ \text{turns lock to lock}$
- $N_2 = 8\ \text{turns lock to lock}$
- $\theta_s = 80^\circ$ (assumed road-wheel sweep, lock to lock)

**To find:**
- (i) steering ratio $i_1$ for $3.5$ turns lock to lock
- (ii) steering ratio $i_2$ for $8$ turns lock to lock

1. Write the relation. Steering ratio is steering-wheel angle divided by road-wheel angle, so for $N$ turns lock to lock and total road-wheel sweep $\theta_s$:
$$i=\frac{360\,N}{\theta_s}$$
2. Assume the road wheels sweep $80^\circ$ lock to lock ($40^\circ$ each side).
3. (i) For $3.5$ turns lock to lock:
$$i_1=\frac{360\,N}{\theta_s}=\frac{360\times3.5}{80}=15.75$$
4. (ii) For $8$ turns lock to lock:
$$i_2=\frac{360\,N}{\theta_s}=\frac{360\times8}{80}=36$$

**Answer:** (i) $i_1 = 15.75 : 1$ for $3.5$ turns ; (ii) $i_2 = 36 : 1$ for $8$ turns (with an $80^\circ$ road-wheel sweep).

**Exam tip:** Write the formula first, state the assumed sweep, substitute, and give the ratio with units ($x : 1$). Marks are lost for not stating the assumption on the sweep angle.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-2c'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a definition of the realigning couple (the torque that straightens the wheel after a turn) and a list of what causes it.

1. Definition: the moment about the steering axis, produced by tyre-road forces and the weight of the vehicle, that tends to return the steered wheels to the straight-ahead position after a turn.
2. For the tyre alone the self-aligning torque is the lateral force times the pneumatic trail:
$$T=F_y\,t_p$$
3. Causes: (1) pneumatic trail, the lateral force resultant acts behind the geometric centre of the contact patch; (2) castor, the contact patch trails behind the steering axis ground point (mechanical trail); (3) kingpin inclination, steering away from straight ahead lifts the body so its weight returns the wheel. Note: scrub radius is not a cause of self-centring; with a brake or drive force it gives a steering disturbance torque.
4. Sketch: a contact patch with the lateral force arrow drawn behind the patch centre, the distance marked as pneumatic trail.
5. Effect: it gives directional stability and driver feel, and makes the steering return to centre after a turn. Too large a couple makes the steering heavy.

**Answer:** Realigning couple $T = F_y \times$ pneumatic trail (plus castor and KPI effects) returns the wheels to straight ahead.

**Exam tip:** Define it in one line, list the causes (pneumatic trail, castor, KPI) and show $T = F_y \times \text{trail}$ with a small patch sketch. The usual slip is naming only one cause.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-2d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants one layout diagram of the whole steering system of a car, from steering wheel to the road wheels, with every part named.

1. Draw a plan view of the car outline: front axle, rear axle, wheelbase $L$ and track marked, so the layout reads as a four wheeler.
2. Steering wheel on the steering shaft inside the column tube, running down and forward to the steering gearbox bolted to the frame side member.
3. Steering gearbox (worm and sector or recirculating ball) gives the speed reduction; its output (rocker) shaft carries the drop arm (Pitman arm), which swings fore and aft.
4. Drag link from the drop arm, with a ball joint at each end, to the steering arm on one stub axle.
5. Stub axles pivoted on kingpins at each end of the front axle beam; each carries a steering arm (track-rod arm).
6. Track rod (tie rod) joining the two steering arms. The arms are angled inward so the arms and track rod form the Ackermann trapezium (inner wheel turns more).
7. Rack and pinion version in one corner: pinion at the lower end of the column meshes with a rack across the car; tie rods with ball joints go from the rack ends to the knuckle arms. No drop arm or drag link is needed.
8. Label all parts: steering wheel, column, gearbox, drop arm, drag link, steering arm, kingpin, stub axle, track rod, ball joints, front and rear axle.

**Answer:** Steering wheel - column - gearbox - drop arm - drag link - steering arm - stub axle, with track rod joining the two front wheels.

**Exam tip:** Show a plan view of the car with the whole chain, front and rear axle and wheel base, every part labelled; add the rack and pinion version if time allows. The usual slip is a side view that hides the track rod.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-2e'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants three line diagrams, one each for rigid axle, double wishbone and semi-trailing arm suspension (printed ''semtrailing''), with the roll centre marked on each.

1. a) Rigid axle on leaf springs: roll centre sits about at the spring-eye or spring-seat height on the centreline (with a Panhard rod, at the height of the rod where it crosses the centreline). Draw axle, springs, body and mark RC.
2. b) Double wishbone: construction as in the 2023 Q2(e) steps below: extend arms to $I$ on each side, join to contact points, intersection is RC.
3. c) Semi-trailing arm: draw the rear view; extend the oblique pivot axis to the transverse plane of the wheel to get the virtual pivot $I$, join $I$ to the contact point on each side, intersection is RC. Add a small plan view of the oblique pivot axis.
4. Mark roll axis and CG.
5. Also asked as 2023 Q2(e) OR (7 marks): schematically represent the roll centre and layout for a double wishbone and a MacPherson strut. Write:
6. Layout, double wishbone (line diagram, front view): body/chassis line, upper and lower wishbone pivoted on the body, outer ball joints joined by the knuckle (upright), wheel on the knuckle, coil spring and damper between the lower arm and the body. Upper arm usually shorter than the lower.
7. Double wishbone: draw both wheels and the body line. Extend the upper and lower wishbone arms of the left side until they meet at the instant centre $I$. Join $I$ to the tyre contact point of the left wheel.
8. Repeat for the right wheel. The two lines cross on the vehicle centre line; that crossing is the roll centre (RC).
9. Layout, MacPherson strut (line diagram, front view): strut (coil spring around the damper) with its top mount on the body and its lower end rigidly fixed to the knuckle, one lower control arm from the body to a ball joint at the bottom of the knuckle, wheel on the knuckle. The steering axis runs from the top mount to the lower ball joint.
10. MacPherson strut: at the top strut mount draw a line perpendicular to the strut, extend the lower control arm line, and their intersection is $I$. Join $I$ to the contact point; repeat on the other side; the crossing is RC.
11. Mark RC, the CG and the roll axis; the arm between CG and roll axis is the roll lever.
12. Label $I$, contact points, ground line and RC.
13. Result to write: a double wishbone RC can be placed by the arm angles anywhere from below ground to a little above it (equal parallel arms put RC at ground level); a MacPherson strut usually gives a higher RC, and it moves as the body rolls.
14. Also asked as MST-I August 2024 Q3 (marks not recovered): roll-centre sketches for leaf spring, double wishbone, MacPherson strut and semi-trailing suspensions. Write:
15. Four small sketches, each with its construction lines and the roll centre RC marked on the centreline: leaf spring is the rigid-axle case, RC at spring-seat or spring-eye height (step a above); double wishbone and MacPherson strut by the instant-centre construction (2023 Q2(e) steps above); semi-trailing arm from the virtual pivot of the oblique axis (step c above).

**Answer:** Three roll-centre line diagrams: rigid axle (at spring/Panhard height), double wishbone and semi-trailing (instant-centre construction). MacPherson strut (asked in 2023 Q2(e) OR and MST-I ''24 Q3): perpendicular to the strut at the top mount meets the extended lower arm at I; join I to the contact point on both sides, and the crossing on the centreline is RC.

**Exam tip:** Draw three small rear views, each with its construction lines and RC marked, and label which is which. The usual slip is using the double wishbone construction for the rigid axle.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-3a'), 1, '**What it asks:** ''Though'' means through. It wants a diagram showing the slip angle: the angle between the wheel''s pointing direction and the direction it really travels.

1. Draw the wheel in plan view as a long rectangle and mark its centreline, the wheel plane.
2. Draw the velocity vector $V$ of the wheel centre, inclined to the wheel plane, and mark the angle between them as the slip angle $\alpha$.
3. Resolve $V$ into $V_x$ along the wheel plane and $V_y$ across it.
$$\tan\alpha=\frac{V_y}{V_x}$$
4. Draw the deformed contact patch with the side force $F_y$ and the opposing cornering reaction, and state that cornering stiffness is the initial slope.
$$F_y\approx C_\alpha\,\alpha$$

**Answer:** Plan view with wheel plane, velocity direction, slip angle $\alpha$, $\tan\alpha = V_y/V_x$, deformed patch and cornering force $F_y \approx C_\alpha\,\alpha$.

**Exam tip:** Draw the plan view with the wheel plane, velocity vector and the angle labelled alpha, then give the formula in one line. Common slip: marking the angle against the vehicle axis instead of the wheel plane.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-3b'), 1, '**What it asks:** ''Breaking'' means braking. It wants the distance the vehicle travels before stopping from 80 km/h when the brakes are only 30 percent efficient (so deceleration is $0.3g$).

**Given:**
- $V = 80\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $\eta = 30\,\% = 0.30$

**To find:**
- stopping distance $s$

1. Convert the speed.
$$v=\frac{V}{3.6}=\frac{80}{3.6}=22.22\ \mathrm{m/s}$$
2. Braking efficiency $\eta = 30\,\%$ means the deceleration is $0.30\,g$.
$$a=\eta\,g=0.30\times9.81=2.943\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$$
3. Kinetic energy equals braking work, $\tfrac12 mv^2=\eta m g\,s$, so the stopping distance is independent of mass.
$$s=\frac{v^2}{2\eta g}=\frac{22.22^2}{2(0.30)(9.81)}=83.9\ \mathrm{m}$$

**Answer:** Stopping distance $s = 83.9\ \mathrm{m}$ (deceleration $a = 2.943\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$).

**Exam tip:** Show each step on its own line: deceleration, speed in m/s, then distance, with units. Common slip: using km/h directly in the equation.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-3c'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the definition of dynamic (effective rolling) radius and the formulas for longitudinal slip and lateral slip.

1. Dynamic (rolling) radius is the radius that relates the forward speed of a freely rolling wheel to its angular speed.
$$r_{dyn}=\frac{V}{\omega}\quad(\text{free rolling})$$
2. Longitudinal slip when driving (wheel faster than the vehicle).
$$s_{drive}=\frac{\omega r_{dyn}-V}{\omega r_{dyn}}$$
3. Longitudinal slip when braking (wheel slower than the vehicle); it is 100 % for a locked wheel.
$$s_{brake}=\frac{V-\omega r_{dyn}}{V}$$
4. Lateral slip: the slip angle $\alpha$ between the wheel plane and the direction of travel.
$$\tan\alpha=\frac{V_y}{V_x}$$
5. Multiply the slip ratios by 100 for percent. The dynamic radius lies between the loaded static radius and the unloaded radius.
6. Also asked as 2024 Q3(a) (2 marks): represent a tyre slip angle through a diagram. Write:
7. Draw the wheel in plan view as a long rectangle and mark its centreline, the wheel plane.
8. Draw the velocity vector $V$ of the wheel centre, inclined to the wheel plane, and mark the angle between them as the slip angle $\alpha$.
9. Resolve $V$ into $V_x$ along the wheel plane and $V_y$ across it.
$$\tan\alpha=\frac{V_y}{V_x}$$
10. Draw the deformed contact patch with the side force $F_y$ and the opposing cornering reaction, and state that cornering stiffness is the initial slope.
$$F_y\approx C_\alpha\,\alpha$$
11. Also asked as Mid Sem Test-II October 2024 Q1 (marks not recovered): dynamic tyre radius, slip angle, contact-patch length and radial, longitudinal and lateral tyre stiffness. Write:
12. Dynamic radius and slip angle exactly as in the steps above (24-3c formulas and the slip-angle diagram), then the extra items:
13. Contact-patch length: for a small radial deflection $\delta$ of a tyre of free radius $r$ the chord on the ground is
$$L\approx 2\sqrt{2r\delta}$$
14. Radial (vertical) stiffness: vertical load per unit radial deflection.
$$k_z=\frac{F_z}{\delta}\ \ (\text{or } dF_z/d\delta)$$
15. Longitudinal stiffness: fore-aft force per unit fore-aft deflection of the tread relative to the rim, $k_x=F_x/\Delta x$; the related slip stiffness is the slope at zero slip.
$$k_x=\frac{F_x}{\Delta x},\quad C_s=\frac{dF_x}{ds}\Big|_{s=0}$$
16. Lateral stiffness: side force per unit sideways deflection, $k_y=F_y/\Delta y$; the related cornering stiffness is the slope against slip angle at zero angle.
$$k_y=\frac{F_y}{\Delta y},\quad C_\alpha=\frac{dF_y}{d\alpha}\Big|_{\alpha=0}$$

**Answer:** $r_{dyn}=V/\omega$; $s_{drive}=(\omega r_{dyn}-V)/(\omega r_{dyn})$; $s_{brake}=(V-\omega r_{dyn})/V$; $\tan\alpha = V_y/V_x$.

**Exam tip:** State dynamic radius, then the two slip formulas with the denominator clearly different for drive and brake. Common slip: using the same denominator for both.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-3d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a labelled tyre cross-section plus tread pattern sketches for off-road, earthmoving and commercial vehicles.

1. Cross-section: draw and label exactly as in 23-3a (tread, belt, carcass, sidewall, bead, liner, rim, with $H$ and $W$).
2. Mark section width $W$, section height $H$ and rim diameter on the section; aspect ratio $=H/W\times100\,\%$.
3. Off-road tread: bold separated blocks with wide open grooves, so the tyre bites into mud and loose ground, self-cleans, and still runs on tarmac.
4. Earthmoving tread: very deep, thick, widely spaced lugs or rock blocks (traction or rock pattern) with a cut-resistant compound, to grip loose soil and rock and resist cutting and chipping at low speed and heavy load.
5. Commercial vehicle tread: circumferential ribs for the steer wheel, transverse bars and blocks for the drive wheel.
6. Label ribs, grooves, blocks and lugs and give one line of duty each.

**Answer:** Labelled cross-section plus off-road, earthmoving and commercial treads with their duties.

**Exam tip:** Draw the cross-section and three treads, each with a duty line. Common slip: drawing the cross-section without labelling the belt and carcass.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-3e'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants three sketches of how the two shoes are mounted in a drum brake: both leading, both trailing, and one leading with one trailing.

1. Draw the drum with a rotation arrow first (forward direction), the backplate, two shoes with linings, the return springs and the actuator.
2. (a) Both leading (two-leading): two single-acting wheel cylinders, one at the toe of each shoe, and each shoe pivots on its own anchor at the opposite end; the drum rotates from each cylinder towards that shoe''s anchor, so in forward rotation both shoes self-energise. In reverse both become trailing.
3. (b) Both trailing (two-trailing): the same two-cylinder, two-anchor layout turned the other way, so the drum rotates from each shoe''s anchor towards its cylinder; both friction moments oppose application, so more input force (usually a servo) is needed, but the brake is stable and less sensitive to lining friction change and fade.
4. (c) Leading-trailing: one double-acting wheel cylinder between the upper shoe tips and a common fixed anchor between the lower ends; one shoe leads and one trails, the roles swap in reverse, so braking is the same both ways (suits rear wheels with the handbrake).
5. Mark the anchor, wheel cylinder, shoes, linings, springs, drum rotation and the friction direction on each shoe.
6. Compare (brake factor for the same input): duo-servo highest, then two-leading, then leading-trailing, two-trailing lowest; higher self-energising means less pedal effort but more sensitivity to lining friction change (less stable).
7. Return springs pull the shoes back off the drum when the pressure is released; an adjuster keeps the lining clearance small as the linings wear.

**Answer:** Three labelled drum layouts: both leading (two wheel cylinders, self-energising), both trailing (de-energising), leading-trailing (one cylinder, one anchor).

**Exam tip:** Draw three clear layouts side by side with the drum rotation arrow, anchors and cylinders labelled, plus one line each. Common slip: forgetting the rotation arrow, which defines leading and trailing.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-4a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the definition of a hydrokinetic (fluid) coupling and how it works: an impeller flings oil onto a turbine so torque passes through fluid, with no mechanical link.

1. Definition: a hydrokinetic (fluid) coupling transmits engine torque through the kinetic energy of a circulating fluid between an engine-driven impeller (pump) and an output turbine (runner), with no mechanical connection.
2. Operation: the impeller, vaned and driven by the engine, throws the oil outward by centrifugal action; the oil crosses to the turbine, strikes its vanes and gives up momentum, turning it; it flows inward and returns to the impeller, a continuous circulation.
3. Torque on the turbine equals torque on the impeller (no stator, so no torque multiplication). Oil circulates only when the impeller runs faster than the turbine, because the difference in centrifugal head drives the vortex flow. So some slip is always needed to transmit torque, and at equal speeds no torque passes:
$$s=\frac{N_p-N_t}{N_p},\quad \eta=\frac{T_t N_t}{T_p N_p}=\frac{N_t}{N_p}=1-s$$
4. At idle the coupling transmits little torque so the car can stand in gear; it smooths take-up and absorbs torsional vibration.

**Answer:** A fluid coupling is an impeller and turbine in an oil-filled casing that transmits torque by oil circulation with a small slip; it gives smooth take-up but no torque multiplication.

**Exam tip:** Draw the two vaned halves in a casing with the oil circulation arrows and label impeller, turbine, casing, input and output shaft; write the slip formula. Common slip: saying the coupling multiplies torque (that is a torque converter, which has an extra stator).') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-4b'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants what torque the starter motor must supply to crank the engine and what speed it needs to turn it, and why (overcoming compression and friction, at a minimum cranking rpm).

1. The starter must turn the crankshaft fast enough for the engine to fire and with enough torque to overcome compression, friction and the viscous drag of cold oil.
2. Torque requirement: the cranking torque at the crankshaft must exceed the resisting torque from compression, bearing and piston friction, and accessory load. It rises with engine size and compression ratio and is highest when the engine is cold.
3. Speed requirement: the engine must be cranked above a minimum speed for ignition and mixture formation, roughly $100$ to $250\ \mathrm{rpm}$ depending on the engine (petrol at the low end, diesel higher because it needs compression heat). It is not one universal value.
4. Gearing: the starter pinion drives the flywheel ring gear with a large reduction (typically about $10:1$ to $15:1$), so a small high-speed motor gives a slow, high-torque crank. With $i = Z_{ring}/Z_{pinion}$:
$$N_{crank}=\frac{N_m}{i},\qquad T_{crank}=i\,T_m\ \text{(losses ignored)}$$
5. Electrical need: the starter draws a very large current from the battery for a short time, so battery capacity and cable size must suit it, and the motor is a series-wound DC motor because it gives its maximum torque at zero speed.
6. After the engine fires, the overrunning clutch lets the pinion free-wheel so the armature is not driven to destructive speed.

**Answer:** Starter needs high cranking torque (more than compression and friction drag, highest when cold) at low crankshaft speed, roughly $100$ to $250\ \mathrm{rpm}$ depending on the engine, got from a high-speed series motor through a pinion to ring-gear reduction of about $10:1$ to $15:1$.

**Exam tip:** Write two headed lines, Torque (must exceed compression + friction + cold-oil drag, highest at a cold start) and Speed (minimum cranking speed, roughly $100$ to $250\ \mathrm{rpm}$ depending on engine, diesel higher). Then give the pinion-to-ring reduction $i = Z_{ring}/Z_{pinion}$ and say why a series motor is used. Common slip: giving the motor''s own rpm as the cranking speed.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-4c'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the dashboard indicators grouped into those for vehicle safety (such as seat belt, airbag, ABS, brake, oil pressure, overheating, battery) and those for vehicle operation (such as fuel, speedometer, tachometer, turn signals, high beam).

1. Use the grouped list of 23-4a and keep the group headings.
2. Vehicle safety/warning: oil pressure, brake, ABS, seat belt, airbag, door ajar, engine overheating, battery/charging fault, engine malfunction (check engine).
3. Vehicle operation: fuel level gauge and low-fuel lamp, speedometer/odometer, tachometer, turn signals, hazard flasher, high-beam, headlamp/parking-lamp on.
4. Add one short function line per item as in 23-4a.

**Answer:** Safety/warning: oil pressure, brake, ABS, seat belt, airbag, door ajar, coolant temperature, battery, engine malfunction (check engine). Operation: fuel gauge, speedometer, tachometer, turn signal, hazard, high beam, headlamp-on.

**Exam tip:** Make two short columns (safety/warning and operation) with 8 to 10 items and a 3 to 5 word function against each; the common slip is writing names only, or calling the battery lamp a charge gauge.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-4d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants block diagrams of the different hybrid arrangements (series, parallel, series-parallel) with a line of explanation for each.

1. Series: draw engine, generator, battery (with power electronics), traction motor, final drive, wheels. Arrows: engine to generator to battery/motor to wheels. No mechanical link engine to wheels.
2. Parallel: draw engine and motor both connected through a coupling to one transmission to the wheels. Battery connected to the motor only.
3. Series-parallel (power split): engine, generator and motor joined through a power-split device so the engine can drive the wheels directly and also charge the battery.
4. Degree of hybridisation: mild hybrid assists the engine but cannot drive on electricity alone; full hybrid can run electric-only for limited conditions; plug-in hybrid also charges from the mains.
5. Regenerative braking: show reverse arrows wheels to motor/generator to battery.
6. Benefit and limitation: better economy and emissions, extra cost and weight.
7. Also asked as 2023 Q4(c) (3 marks): describe in brief the hybrid power drive. Write:
8. Definition: a hybrid electric vehicle combines an internal combustion engine with one or more electric machines and an energy store (battery), so each is used where it is most efficient.
9. Power drive in a series hybrid: engine drives a generator, electricity goes to the battery or to the traction motor, and only the traction motor turns the wheels. The engine is not mechanically connected to the wheels.
10. Power drive in a parallel hybrid: engine and electric motor both have mechanical paths to the wheels through a coupling and the transmission, and can drive together or separately.
11. Regenerative braking: while slowing, the motor acts as a generator, converting part of the vehicle kinetic energy to electricity stored in the battery.
12. Benefits: better fuel economy and lower emissions, engine run near its efficient point, stop-start and electric-only low-speed running.
13. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Energy storage and other electric concepts.** Battery (Li-ion, NiMH) and supercapacitor store energy. Battery electric vehicle (BEV) uses only motor and battery; fuel cell electric vehicle (FCEV) makes electricity from hydrogen and emits only water.

**Answer:** Three architectures (series, parallel, series-parallel) drawn as block schematics, classified by degree as mild, full or plug-in, with regenerative braking.

**Exam tip:** Draw block diagrams with arrows (engine, generator, battery, motor, wheels) and label power flow; 3 to 5 marks want definition plus series and parallel in two or three lines each plus regen braking. Common slip: forgetting that in series the engine has no mechanical link to the wheels.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-4e'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a labelled sketch of a single plate dry clutch, showing the parts that clamp and release the friction disc.

1. Reproduce the answer of 23-4d: draw the section in the same order (flywheel, cover, pressure plate and spring, driven plate with two linings on the splined hub, release bearing and fork, input shaft).
2. Add the torque-path arrows, the two-line working and the torque formula (no numbers are asked). Here z = number of friction surfaces, mu = coefficient of friction, W = total axial spring force on the plate (N), R_m = mean friction radius (m). For uniform wear (worn-in clutch, the usual design assumption) $R_m=\frac{r_1+r_2}{2}$. For uniform pressure (new clutch) $R_m=\frac{2}{3}\,\frac{r_1^3-r_2^3}{r_1^2-r_2^2}$, where $r_1$ and $r_2$ are the outer and inner lining radii.
$$T=z\,\mu\,W\,R_m$$
3. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Coil spring versus diaphragm spring clutch.** Coil type uses 6 to 9 springs round the pressure plate with release levers. Diaphragm type uses one dished steel plate as spring and lever: lighter, compact, gives uniform pressure and needs less pedal effort, so it is now common in cars.
4. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Cone clutch.** Friction surfaces are conical (cone angle about 12 to 15 degrees), giving a wedging action and higher torque than a flat plate for the same spring force. It is an older design, now rare because it is hard to release cleanly.
5. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Clutch pedal free play.** About 10 to 15 mm of pedal travel is kept before the release bearing touches the levers, so the clutch is fully engaged. Too little free play causes slip, too much causes drag.

**Answer:** Single plate dry clutch: flywheel on the crankshaft; driven plate with a friction lining on both faces, cushion segments and a torsional-damper hub on the splined gearbox input shaft (spigot in the crank pilot bush); pressure plate loaded by a diaphragm spring (or coil springs) inside a cover bolted to the flywheel; release bearing, fork and pedal linkage. Pedal up: the spring clamps the disc and torque passes by friction, $T=z\mu W R_m$ with $z=2$ and $R_m=(r_1+r_2)/2$ (uniform wear). Pedal down: the clamp is released and the drive is broken.

**Exam tip:** Draw a clean sectional half-view in the order flywheel, friction disc, pressure plate, spring, cover, release bearing and fork, input shaft, and label every part; add two lines on working. Common slip: one friction face instead of two, or missing the splined hub.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-5a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants two short definitions: what a splitter gear is and what a range change gear is, both being ways of getting more ratios in a heavy-vehicle gearbox.

1. **Splitter gear:** an auxiliary gear stage with a small ratio step (typically about 1.2 to 1.3, roughly the square root of the main gear step) placed in series with the main gearbox. Each main ratio can be used in a high or a low splitter position, giving two closely spaced ratios and smaller steps between gears, so a heavily loaded truck keeps its engine in the useful speed range after a shift.
2. **Range change gear:** an auxiliary gear stage with a large ratio step that shifts the whole set of main gearbox ratios to a low range (for hard work, steep climbs) or a high range (for road running). It extends the span of ratios rather than refining them.
3. Combination: a four-speed main box with a two-speed splitter and a two-speed range gives $4\times2\times2 = 16$ combinations (usable ratios depend on the design).
4. Both are auxiliary gearboxes placed in series with the main gearbox: the splitter refines the steps, the range gear widens the total spread. The splitter is normally mounted ahead of the main gearbox (input side) and the range-change unit behind it (output side), often as an epicyclic set.

**Answer:** Splitter: auxiliary stage with a small step, halves the spacing of the main ratios. Range change: auxiliary stage with a large step, moves all main ratios into a low or high group.

**Exam tip:** Define each in 2 lines, state the effect (finer steps versus wider spread) and give the combination count. Slip: describing both as the same thing.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-5b'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a simple line sketch of the propeller shaft system of a rear-wheel-drive car: gearbox, universal joints, shaft, slip joint, differential and rear axle in order.

1. Draw the engine, clutch and gearbox at the front as one unit fixed to the frame (side view).
2. From the gearbox output shaft draw the first universal joint, then the propeller shaft, then the second universal joint at the rear.
3. Draw a splined slip joint (sliding sleeve) at one end of the propeller shaft: it takes up the change in length as the axle moves.
4. Connect the second joint to the pinion shaft of the final drive and differential on the rear axle, with the half shafts and wheels.
5. Show the axle moving up and down by a dotted outline to explain the angle change. For a long shaft add a two-piece shaft with a centre bearing.
6. Label: engine, clutch, gearbox, UJ1, slip joint, propeller shaft, UJ2, final drive/differential, half shafts, rear wheels.

**Answer:** Engine, clutch, gearbox, universal joint, slip-jointed propeller shaft, universal joint, final drive and differential, half shafts, rear wheels.

**Exam tip:** Draw a side view with engine, clutch, gearbox, joint, shaft, slip joint, joint, rear axle, and label each. Slip: omitting the slip joint or one of the two universal joints.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-5c'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the total pulling force the wheels must deliver at a steady 60 km/h on a flat road. At constant speed this equals the resistances, so add rolling resistance $C_{rr}W$ and air drag $\tfrac12\rho C_d A v^2$. No gradient or acceleration term.

**Given:**
- $m = 4\ \mathrm{t}$
- $A = 4\ \mathrm{m^2}$
- $C_r = 0.005$
- $C_d = 0.4$
- $V = 60\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $\rho = 0.8\ \mathrm{kg/m^3}$

**To find:**
- tractive force $F$

1. Convert units.
$$v=\frac{V}{3.6}=\frac{60}{3.6}=16.67\ \mathrm{m/s},\quad m=4000\ \mathrm{kg}$$
2. Rolling resistance.
$$F_r = C_r\, m g = 0.005(4000)(9.81) = 196.20\ \mathrm{N}$$
3. Aerodynamic drag.
$$F_a = \tfrac12 \rho\, C_d A v^2 = 0.5(0.8)(0.4)(4)(16.67)^2 = 177.78\ \mathrm{N}$$
4. Tractive force.
$$F = F_r + F_a = 373.98\ \mathrm{N}$$
5. Corresponding road power.
$$P = F v = 6.23\ \mathrm{kW}$$

**Answer:** Tractive force $F \approx 374\ \mathrm{N}$ (rolling $196\ \mathrm{N}$ + aerodynamic $178\ \mathrm{N}$); road power $\approx 6.23\ \mathrm{kW}$.

**Exam tip:** Compute rolling and air drag separately, add them, then multiply by speed for power. Slip: keeping speed in km/h or forgetting the half in the air drag term.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-5d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants two parts: (1) a labelled sketch of the whole differential assembly, and (2) an explanation of how the crown wheel and pinion are adjusted so that the teeth mesh correctly (backlash and contact pattern).

1. Draw the assembly (plan view): propeller shaft flange driving the bevel pinion; the crown wheel meshing with it at right angles; the differential cage bolted to the crown wheel; cross-pin (spider) carrying two planet pinions inside the cage; two side gears splined to the half shafts; bearings supporting cage and pinion; the casing.
2. Working in one line: crown wheel turns the cage and cross-pin; in straight running the planets do not spin; in a turn they spin on the cross-pin so one wheel gains the speed the other loses, $n_L + n_R = 2 n_{cage}$.
3. Tooth contact method: first set pinion depth and bearing preload with shims; set crown wheel position and backlash with the side adjusters; coat teeth with an approved marking compound; rotate under light braking in both directions and read the contact patch.
4. Read the pattern: pinion depth mainly moves the contact in tooth height (face/flank), crown wheel position moves it along the tooth length (heel/toe). Adjust until the patch is well spread, centred on the flank and away from the edges, then recheck backlash and preload.
5. Corrections: heavy face (high) contact, move the pinion in towards the crown wheel axis (deeper). Heavy flank (low) contact, move the pinion out. Heavy toe contact, move the crown wheel away from the pinion (this increases backlash). Heavy heel contact, move the crown wheel towards the pinion (this reduces backlash). After each move, recheck backlash (typically about 0.15 to 0.25 mm, see the service manual) and bearing preload.
6. Draw one tooth showing face, flank, heel and toe with the correct contact patch shaded. Exact pattern and limits come from the service manual.
7. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Limited-slip differential.** Clutch plates or a viscous coupling inside the differential resist speed difference between the two wheels, so when one wheel is on ice or mud, torque still reaches the wheel with grip. A plain differential sends equal torque, limited by the weaker wheel.
8. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Differential lock.** A dog clutch (manual or electric) locks the two axle shafts together so both wheels turn at the same speed, giving full traction in mud or off-road; it should be disengaged on tarmac and in turns to avoid tyre scrub and transmission wind-up.
9. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Final drive gear types.** Spiral bevel crown wheel and pinion have axes meeting; hypoid gears offset the pinion below the crown wheel centre, giving a lower propeller shaft and floor, stronger teeth and quieter running, but they need special hypoid oil.

**Answer:** Assembly: bevel pinion, crown wheel, cage, cross-pin, planet pinions, side gears, half shafts, bearings. Correct tooth contact is set by pinion depth shims and crown wheel adjusters, checked with marking compound and backlash.

**Exam tip:** Draw the assembly clearly with at least 8 labels, add one line on working and a short note on contact pattern adjustment. Slip: mixing up pinion and crown wheel or leaving out the cage.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '24-5e'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the ratio of each of 4 forward gears plus the final drive. Steps: find engine torque from $T = 9549\,P/N$ (P in kW, N in rpm; equivalently $T = 60P/(2\pi N)$ with P in W) $= 9549\times75/4000 = 179.05\ \mathrm{N\,m}$, then final drive from top speed in top gear, bottom gear ratio from the gradeability or tractive need at full load (net weight plus 4 ton payload), and intermediate ratios in geometric progression.

**Given:**
- $m_{veh} = 2000\ \mathrm{kg}$
- $m_{load} = 4000\ \mathrm{kg}$
- $V_{max} = 120\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $r = 425\ \mathrm{mm}$
- $P = 75\ \mathrm{kW}$ at $N = 4000\ \mathrm{rpm}$
- 4 forward gears

**To find:**
- (i) final drive ratio $i_f$
- (ii) gearbox ratios $i_1, i_2, i_3, i_4$

1. Engine torque from peak power.
$$T = \frac{P}{\omega} = \frac{75000}{2\pi(4000)/60} = 179.05\ \mathrm{N\,m}$$
2. Gross mass.
$$m=m_{veh}+m_{load}=2000+4000=6000\ \mathrm{kg}$$
3. (i) Final drive from direct top gear.
$$v_{max}=\frac{V}{3.6}=\frac{120}{3.6}=33.33\ \mathrm{m/s},\quad i_f=\frac{2\pi r N}{60\,v_{max}}=\frac{2\pi(0.425)(4000)}{60(33.33)}=5.3407$$
4. (ii) Road load at low speed, grade 1 in 5.
$$F=mg(\sin\theta+C_r\cos\theta),\quad \tan\theta=0.2\ \Rightarrow\ F=12409.2\ \mathrm{N}$$
5. (ii) First gear.
$$i_1 = \frac{F\, r}{T\, i_f\, \eta} = \frac{12409.2(0.425)}{179.05(5.3407)(0.85)} = 6.4884$$
6. (ii) Spacing with $i_4 = 1$.
$$K=\Big(\frac{i_4}{i_1}\Big)^{1/3}=(1/6.4884)^{1/3}=0.5362,\quad i_2=i_1K=3.4788,\quad i_3=i_1K^2=1.8651$$
7. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Overdrive and direct top gear.** Many gearboxes use a top gear with ratio 1 (direct drive) and a fifth gear with ratio below 1 (overdrive) that lowers engine rpm at cruise, saving fuel and noise at the cost of less tractive effort for hills.
8. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Gearbox needs and spacing.** A gearbox is needed because an IC engine gives torque only over a limited speed range and cannot reverse. Ratios should be closer at the top, widening towards low gears, so shifts feel even; the number of gears depends on vehicle type (heavy trucks use 5 to 16).
9. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Road load terms for the tractive-effort check.** $R_r = f\,W$, $R_a = \tfrac{1}{2}\rho\,C_d\,A\,v^2$, $R_g = W\sin\theta$; the available tractive effort must exceed their sum, and be at most $\mu W_{drive}$ to avoid wheel slip.

**Answer:** (i) final drive $i_f = 5.34:1$; (ii) gearbox ratios $i_1 = 6.49$, $i_2 = 3.48$, $i_3 = 1.87$, $i_4 = 1.00$.

**Exam tip:** Show every step with its formula and end with a table of ratios. Slip: forgetting the loaded mass in the first-gear calculation.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-1a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a simple labelled sketch of a vehicle chassis (the frame that carries everything). Draw the rails, cross-members and mark where engine, axles and body sit.

1. Draw two long side members (rails) as parallel lines in plan view.
2. Join them with 4 to 5 cross members so the shape is a ladder.
3. Front end: engine on the frame, with flywheel and clutch behind it, then the gearbox.
4. Behind the gearbox draw the propeller shaft running back to the differential on the rear axle.
5. Draw the front axle with steering at the front and the rear axle with wheels; label side member, cross member, engine, clutch, gearbox, propeller shaft, differential, front axle, rear axle.

**Answer:** Ladder-type conventional chassis with the power path engine, flywheel, clutch, gearbox, propeller shaft, differential, rear axle labelled.

**Exam tip:** Draw a clean plan-view ladder (2 side rails, 4-5 cross members), put engine to gearbox to propeller shaft to rear axle in order, and label 6-8 parts. Slip: forgetting the labels or drawing the shaft ending before the differential.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-1b'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a simple diagram showing the three rigid-body rotations of a car body, each about its own axis, with the axis named and an arrow for the motion.

1. Draw a car outline in side or isometric view with the CG marked.
2. Mark the x axis forward (longitudinal), y axis sideways (transverse) and z axis vertical through the CG.
3. Roll: curved arrow about the x axis; body leans sideways, as in a corner.
4. Pitch: curved arrow about the y axis; nose dives in braking, nose lifts and rear squats in acceleration.
5. Yaw: curved arrow about the z axis, seen from above; the car turns or spins about the vertical.

**Answer:** Roll is rotation about the longitudinal axis, pitch about the transverse axis, yaw about the vertical axis.

**Exam tip:** Draw one car with x, y, z axes through the CG and a curved arrow for each motion, labelled with the axis name. Slip: swapping roll and pitch axes.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-1c'), 1, '**What it asks:** Find how many times per second the wheel is pushed up and down by the wavy road, at three speeds. Convert km/h to m/s, then divide speed by the 20 m wavelength.

**Given:**
- $V_1 = 20\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $V_2 = 40\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $V_3 = 60\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $\lambda = 20\ \mathrm{m}$

**To find:**
- (i) road-input frequency $f$ at $20\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- (ii) $f$ at $40\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- (iii) $f$ at $60\ \mathrm{km/h}$

1. Convert speeds to m/s.
$$v=\frac{V}{3.6}:\ \frac{20}{3.6}=5.556,\ \frac{40}{3.6}=11.11,\ \frac{60}{3.6}=16.67\ \mathrm{m/s}$$
2. Apply $f = v/\lambda$ with $\lambda = 20\ \mathrm{m}$.
$$f=\frac{v}{\lambda}$$
3. (i) 20 km/h.
$$f=\frac{v}{\lambda}=\frac{5.556}{20}=0.278\ \mathrm{Hz}$$
4. (ii) 40 km/h.
$$f=\frac{v}{\lambda}=\frac{11.11}{20}=0.556\ \mathrm{Hz}$$
5. (iii) 60 km/h.
$$f=\frac{v}{\lambda}=\frac{16.67}{20}=0.833\ \mathrm{Hz}$$

**Answer:** (i) $f = 0.278\ \mathrm{Hz}$ at $20\ \mathrm{km/h}$; (ii) $f = 0.556\ \mathrm{Hz}$ at $40\ \mathrm{km/h}$; (iii) $f = 0.833\ \mathrm{Hz}$ at $60\ \mathrm{km/h}$ (frequency rises in proportion to speed; $60\ \mathrm{km/h}$ approaches the $1\ \mathrm{Hz}$ body-bounce region).

**Exam tip:** Show km/h to m/s (divide by 3.6), write $f = v/\lambda$, then substitute for every speed. Slip: using km/h directly in the formula.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-1d'), 1, '**What it asks:** Find how much load moves from the rear wheels to the front (or back) in two cases: braking at 0.2g, and travelling on a 1 in 10 slope. Use the c.g. height and the wheelbase to get the transfer in each case.

**Given:**
- $m = 1400\ \mathrm{kg}$
- $l = 2.5\ \mathrm m$
- $h = 0.5\ \mathrm m$
- $a = 1.0\ \mathrm m$ (CG from front wheel)
- braking deceleration $= 0.2g$
- gradient $1$ in $10$

**To find:**
- (a) weight transfer $\Delta W$ when braking at $0.2g$
- (b) weight transfer when travelling on a gradient of 1 in 10

1. Data: $W = mg$, $a$ = CG distance from the front wheel, $b = l - a$.
$$W=mg=1400\times9.81=13734\ \mathrm N,\ \ b=l-a,\ \ l=2.5,\ a=1.0,\ b=1.5,\ h=0.5\ \mathrm m$$
2. Static axle loads.
$$R_f=\frac{Wb}{l}=\frac{13734\times1.5}{2.5}=8240\ \mathrm N,\quad R_r=\frac{Wa}{l}=5494\ \mathrm N$$
3. (a) (a) Braking at $0.2g$: load transfer rear to front.
$$\Delta W=\frac{W(a_x/g)h}{l}=\frac{13734\times0.2\times0.5}{2.5}=549\ \mathrm N$$
4. (a) Dynamic loads while braking.
$$R_f''=R_f+\Delta W=8240+549=8790\ \mathrm N,\quad R_r''=R_r-\Delta W=5494-549=4944\ \mathrm N$$
5. (b) (b) Gradient 1 in 10: $\tan\theta = 0.1$.
$$\tan\theta=0.1:\ \cos\theta=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+\tan^2\theta}}=0.9950,\ \sin\theta=\cos\theta\tan\theta=0.0995$$
6. (b) Axle loads on the slope.
$$R_f=\frac{W(b\cos\theta-h\sin\theta)}{l}=7926\ \mathrm N,\quad R_r=\frac{W(a\cos\theta+h\sin\theta)}{l}=5740\ \mathrm N$$
7. (b) Weight transfer to the rear due to the slope, and change against the level static loads.
$$\frac{W h\sin\theta}{l}=273\ \mathrm N;\quad R_f:\ 8240\to7926\ (-314\ \mathrm N),\quad R_r:\ 5494\to5740\ (+246\ \mathrm N)$$
8. (b) Check: axle loads add to the normal component of weight.
$$R_f+R_r=W\cos\theta:\ 7926+5740=13666=W\cos\theta$$

**Answer:** (a) Braking at $0.2g$: $\Delta W = 549\ \mathrm N$ moves from rear to front; $R_f = 8790\ \mathrm N$, $R_r = 4944\ \mathrm N$. (b) Climbing 1 in 10: weight transfer $W h\sin\theta/l = 273\ \mathrm N$ from front to rear; $R_f = 7926\ \mathrm N$, $R_r = 5740\ \mathrm N$ (against level ground the front falls $314\ \mathrm N$ and the rear rises $246\ \mathrm N$, because $W\cos\theta$ is also smaller).

**Exam tip:** Write static loads first, then the transfer formula, then the new axle loads, and check front plus rear equals the weight. Slip: adding the transfer to the wrong axle.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-1e'), 1, '**What it asks:** Two things: draw the three-point engine mount (engine held at three support points on the frame), and give the static deflection the mount should have. ''n = 30/root x'' means natural frequency n in cycles per minute equals 30 divided by the square root of x, so you pick a target n and solve for x.

**Given:**
- $n = 30/\sqrt{x}$ with $n$ in cycles/min and $x$ in m

**To find:**
- (i) schematic of a 3-point engine mount
- (ii) static deflection $x$ of the mount

1. (i) Sketch (about 4 marks): plan view of engine and gearbox on the frame.
2. (i) Two mounts at the front, one on each side of the engine; one mount at the rear under the gearbox or bell housing; the three points form a triangle.
3. (i) Show the line through the front mounts near the engine roll axis, with rubber pad or V-mount shown at each point and a note on torque reaction being absorbed.
4. (ii) Deflection (about 3 marks). The paper gives no n, so choose it from the disturbing frequency: 4-cylinder 4-stroke at 750 rpm idle fires twice per revolution.
$$n_{dist}=2N=2\times750=1500\ \mathrm{cpm}$$
5. (ii) Mount frequency at one third of the disturbing frequency (frequency ratio 3).
$$n=\frac{n_{dist}}{r}=\frac{1500}{3}=500\ \mathrm{cpm}$$
6. (ii) Rearrange $n = 30/\sqrt{x}$, x in metres.
$$x=\left(\frac{30}{n}\right)^2=\left(\frac{30}{500}\right)^2=0.0036\ \mathrm m=3.6\ \mathrm{mm}$$
7. (ii) Cross-check with the SI formula.
$$f=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{g}{x}}=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{9.81}{0.0036}}=8.31\ \mathrm{Hz}=498.5\ \mathrm{cpm}$$
8. Also asked as 2024 Q1(b) (2 marks): represent the natural frequency of a spring suspension in terms of static deflection. Write:
9. At equilibrium the spring force balances the weight.
$$mg=k\delta\ \Rightarrow\ \frac{k}{m}=\frac{g}{\delta}$$
10. Substitute in the natural frequency.
$$f_n=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{k}{m}}=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{g}{\delta}}\ \mathrm{Hz}$$
11. With $\delta$ in metres this is about $0.5/\sqrt{\delta}\ \mathrm{Hz}$, or $30/\sqrt{\delta}$ cycles per minute (the form used in 2023 Q1(e)).
12. Also asked as 2024 Q1(a) (2 marks): represent a rubber pad type engine mount through a sketch. Write:
13. Draw the frame cross member at the bottom with a flat bracket on it.
14. Place the rubber pad on it, bonded between two steel plates (top and bottom).
15. Put the engine bracket on the top plate and show the through-bolt with a rebound washer limiting upward movement.
16. Label engine bracket, steel plates, rubber pad, frame bracket, bolt, and arrows for compression (weight) and shear (torque reaction).
17. Also asked as 2025 Q1(a) (2 marks): schematically represent a flexible rubber engine mount. Write: the rubber pad mount sketch from the 2024 Q1(a) steps above (engine bracket, steel plates, rubber pad, frame bracket, bolt, rebound washer), then optionally:
18. Optionally add a V-mount: two inclined rubber blocks with an interleaf plate, meeting under the engine bracket, so load is shared between compression and shear.
19. Label rubber, steel plate, interleaf plate, engine bracket, frame bracket and the compression and shear arrows.

**Answer:** (i) Three-point mount: two at the front, one at the rear under the gearbox, forming a triangle near the roll axis. (ii) Static deflection $x = (30/n)^2$; for the assumed idle case $n = 500\ \mathrm{cpm}$ this gives $x = 3.6\ \mathrm{mm}$.

**Exam tip:** Give a neat plan view with 2 front and 1 rear mount labelled, then the deflection calculation with the assumed n stated clearly. Slip: not stating the assumption for n.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-2a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a front-view sketch of a wheel showing king pin inclination (king pin leaning inwards from vertical) and a side-view showing caster angle (king pin leaning backwards or forwards), with the angles marked and named.

1. Front view: draw the wheel as a rectangle on the ground line, the axle end and a steering axis (kingpin axis) through the wheel centre region, tilted so the top leans inward. Mark the angle between this axis and the vertical as kingpin inclination (KPI).
2. Side view: draw the wheel circle with hub centre, and the steering axis tilted so the top leans backward. Mark the angle to the vertical as castor, and mark the point where the axis meets the ground ahead of the contact patch (castor trail).
3. Label: steering axis, vertical, KPI, castor, direction of travel arrow, contact patch.

**Answer:** KPI = inward tilt of the steering axis from vertical (front view). Castor = backward tilt of the steering axis from vertical (side view), giving positive trail.

**Exam tip:** Draw two neat views side by side (front and side) with the steering axis dashed and the vertical dotted; label KPI, castor, trail and direction of travel. About half a page earns full marks; the usual slip is measuring the angle from the horizontal or putting castor in the front view.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-2b'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the target natural frequency range for a suspension spring (the value that gives a comfortable ride, about 1 to 1.5 Hz) and a line on why that is chosen.

1. Desired ride (body bounce) frequency of the suspension spring is about $1$ to $1.5\ \mathrm{Hz}$, close to a walking pace of the human body.
2. Rear suspension is usually made slightly stiffer in frequency ($10$ to $20$ percent higher) than front so the body pitching dies out quickly.
3. Static deflection for $1\ \mathrm{Hz}$ follows from $f=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{g/x}$:
$$x=\frac{g}{(2\pi f)^2}=\frac{9.81}{(2\pi\times1)^2}=0.248\ \mathrm{m}$$
4. The unsprung wheel-hop mode is a different, higher frequency (about $10$ to $12\ \mathrm{Hz}$) and is not the desired ride frequency.
5. Why the sprung mass is tuned to this range: below about $1\ \mathrm{Hz}$ people feel floaty and can get motion sick; above about $2\ \mathrm{Hz}$ the ride feels harsh.

**Answer:** About $1$ to $1.5\ \mathrm{Hz}$ (static deflection about $0.25\ \mathrm{m}$ at $1\ \mathrm{Hz}$).

**Exam tip:** Write $1$ to $1.5\ \mathrm{Hz}$, give the reason (matches walking), mention rear slightly higher than front, and add the static deflection relation. Two marks needs about two to three crisp points; the slip is quoting the wheel-hop frequency ($10$ to $12\ \mathrm{Hz}$) instead.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-2c'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a sketch of the rear axle with a coil spring fitted, showing where the spring sits between axle and body, plus the damper and locating links if you can.

1. Rear view: draw the axle beam with a wheel at each end, a coil spring standing on a spring seat on the axle above each hub, upper seat on the body, and a telescopic damper beside each spring.
2. Add a Panhard rod from the axle on one side to the body on the other side to take lateral force.
3. Side view: add a trailing (or torque) arm from the axle to a rubber-bushed body bracket to take drive and brake force and torque reaction.
4. Label: body, coil spring, spring seat, damper, axle, trailing arm, Panhard rod, wheel.

**Answer:** Rigid rear axle on coil springs, located by trailing arms (longitudinal) and a Panhard rod (lateral), with dampers alongside the springs.

**Exam tip:** Draw a rear view with axle, springs, dampers and Panhard rod, and a small side view with the trailing arm; label every part. The usual slip is drawing only springs and forgetting the links that locate the axle.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-2d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants sketches of the steering linkages of a car, from steering wheel to the wheels: column, gearbox, drop (Pitman) arm, drag link, steering arms and track rod for a beam axle, plus the split linkage (centre link, idler arm, tie rods) and rack and pinion used with independent suspension, all labelled.

1. Draw the plan view of the front axle: axle beam with a kingpin and stub axle at each end, a steering arm on each stub axle angled slightly inward.
2. Join the two steering arms with the track rod (tie rod). With the track rod behind the axle the arms angle inward (their lines, extended, meet near the centre of the rear axle), so the track rod is shorter than the kingpin spacing and forms the Ackermann trapezium. If the track rod is ahead of the axle the arms must angle outward instead.
3. Draw the chain: steering wheel, steering column, steering gearbox, drop arm (Pitman arm) swinging in a plane, drag link to the steering arm of one stub axle.
4. Mark ball joints at the ends of the drag link and track rod, and the kingpins at the axle ends.
5. Third linkage, for independent front suspension: the single track rod is split. The drop arm moves a centre (relay) link supported at its other end by an idler arm pivoted on the frame, and two short tie rods with ball joints run from the centre link to the steering arms, so each wheel can rise and fall without steering the other.
6. Second small sketch: rack and pinion, pinion on the column, rack across the car, tie rods with ball joints to the steering arms on the knuckles.
7. Label every part.

**Answer:** Three linkages: (1) beam axle: steering wheel - column - gearbox - drop arm - drag link - steering arm - stub axle, with a track rod joining both arms (Ackermann trapezium); (2) independent suspension: drop arm - centre link - idler arm, with two short tie rods to the steering arms; (3) rack and pinion: pinion - rack - tie rods - knuckle arms.

**Exam tip:** Draw a clear plan view with the steering chain in sequence and every part labelled, plus a small rack and pinion sketch if time allows. Typical answer is one full sketch with 8 to 10 labels; the usual slip is leaving out the track rod or the ball joints.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-2e'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants two line diagrams, one for a double wishbone and one for a MacPherson strut (the paper spells it ''Mac person''), each with the roll centre located by construction lines.

1. Layout, double wishbone (line diagram, front view): body/chassis line, upper and lower wishbone pivoted on the body, outer ball joints joined by the knuckle (upright), wheel on the knuckle, coil spring and damper between the lower arm and the body. Upper arm usually shorter than the lower.
2. Double wishbone: draw both wheels and the body line. Extend the upper and lower wishbone arms of the left side until they meet at the instant centre $I$. Join $I$ to the tyre contact point of the left wheel.
3. Repeat for the right wheel. The two lines cross on the vehicle centre line; that crossing is the roll centre (RC).
4. Layout, MacPherson strut (line diagram, front view): strut (coil spring around the damper) with its top mount on the body and its lower end rigidly fixed to the knuckle, one lower control arm from the body to a ball joint at the bottom of the knuckle, wheel on the knuckle. The steering axis runs from the top mount to the lower ball joint.
5. MacPherson strut: at the top strut mount draw a line perpendicular to the strut, extend the lower control arm line, and their intersection is $I$. Join $I$ to the contact point; repeat on the other side; the crossing is RC.
6. Mark RC, the CG and the roll axis; the arm between CG and roll axis is the roll lever.
7. Label $I$, contact points, ground line and RC.
8. Result to write: a double wishbone RC can be placed by the arm angles anywhere from below ground to a little above it (equal parallel arms put RC at ground level); a MacPherson strut usually gives a higher RC, and it moves as the body rolls.

**Answer:** Two line diagrams (double wishbone, MacPherson strut) with the layout drawn and the roll centre found on each: RC = intersection on the centreline of the lines joining each contact point to its wheel''s instant centre. MacPherson RC is usually higher than double wishbone RC.

**Exam tip:** Draw the construction to scale with arms extended as dashed lines, mark the instant centre $I$, the contact points and RC clearly for each suspension type. The usual slip is joining $I$ to the wheel centre instead of the contact point.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-3a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a labelled cross-section sketch of a tyre (tread, belt, carcass, sidewall, bead) and the definition of aspect ratio as section height over section width.

1. Draw the transverse section as an inverted U around the rim: thick tread at the top, bracing belt (crown plies) just below it, carcass plies running down both sidewalls and wrapping round the beads.
2. Draw the two steel-wire beads seated on the rim flanges, the inner liner (tubeless) lining the inside, and the enclosed air space.
3. Mark the dimensions: section width $W$ (across the sidewalls), section height $H$ (bead seat to tread crown), rim diameter $D_r$.
4. Aspect ratio is section height divided by section width, as a percentage.
$$\text{aspect ratio}=\frac{H}{W}\times100\,\%$$
5. Example: in 245/75, $H = 0.75\times245 = 183.75\ \mathrm{mm}$. A lower aspect ratio means a shorter sidewall relative to the width.

**Answer:** Aspect ratio $= (H/W)\times100\,\%$ (section height over section width); sketch with tread, belt, carcass, sidewall, bead and liner, and the dimensions $H$ and $W$ labelled.

**Exam tip:** Draw a large clean cross-section with tread, belt, carcass plies, sidewall and bead labelled with arrows, then one line for aspect ratio; about a page. Common slip: forgetting the bead or writing the ratio upside down.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-3b'), 1, '**What it asks:** ''Breaking'' is a typo for braking. It wants the definition of braking efficiency: how much of the possible deceleration (in g) the brakes actually achieve, as a ratio or percentage.

1. Braking efficiency is the braking force actually developed at the road, expressed as a percentage of the vehicle weight.
$$\eta=\frac{F_b}{mg}\times100\,\%$$
2. On a level road with no other resistance the braking force is $ma$, so the fractional value is the deceleration in units of $g$.
$$\eta=\frac{a}{g}$$
3. It is limited by tyre-road adhesion (at most the friction coefficient $\mu$), and it is not the thermal efficiency of the brake or the pad friction coefficient.

**Answer:** Braking efficiency $=\dfrac{F_b}{mg}\times100\,\%$ (braking force over vehicle weight) $= a/g$ on a level road; an adhesion-limited figure, not a thermal efficiency.

**Exam tip:** Give the definition, the formula and one line on the factors that reduce it; about half a page. Common slip: stating the formula without defining the symbols.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-3c'), 1, '**What it asks:** It gives a tyre marking 245/75 R16 and wants you to decode it and calculate the tyre radii (the unloaded or free radius from rim diameter plus sidewall height).

**Given:**
- $W = 245\ \mathrm{mm}$ (section width)
- $AR = 75\,\%$ (aspect ratio)
- $D_{in} = 16\ \mathrm{in}$ (rim diameter)

**To find:**
- (i) free outer radius $r_{out}$
- (ii) static loaded radius $r_s$
- (iii) dynamic rolling radius $r_d$

1. Read the designation: 245 is the nominal section width in mm, 75 is the aspect ratio in %, R means radial construction, 16 is the rim diameter in inches. R does not mean radius.
2. Convert the rim diameter to mm.
$$D_r=D_{in}\times25.4=16\times25.4=406.4\ \mathrm{mm},\quad r_{rim}=\frac{D_r}{2}=203.2\ \mathrm{mm}$$
3. Section height from the aspect ratio.
$$H=AR\times W=0.75\times245=183.75\ \mathrm{mm}$$
4. (i) Nominal unloaded outer diameter and radius.
$$D=D_r+2H=406.4+2(183.75)=773.9\ \mathrm{mm},\quad r_{out}=\frac{D}{2}=386.95\ \mathrm{mm}$$
5. (ii) Static loaded radius, with a typical ratio (assumed, not given): $k_s \approx 0.96$ of the free radius.
$$r_s\approx k_s\,r_{out}=0.96\times386.95=371.5\ \mathrm{mm}$$
6. (iii) Dynamic rolling radius, with a typical ratio (assumed, not given): $k_d \approx 0.98$ of the free radius.
$$r_d\approx k_d\,r_{out}=0.98\times386.95=379.2\ \mathrm{mm}$$

**Answer:** (i) Free outer radius $r_{out} = 386.95\ \mathrm{mm}$ ($0.387\ \mathrm{m}$), from rim radius $203.2\ \mathrm{mm}$, section height $183.75\ \mathrm{mm}$, outer diameter $773.9\ \mathrm{mm}$; (ii) static loaded radius $r_s \approx 371.5\ \mathrm{mm}$ (assumed $0.96$); (iii) dynamic rolling radius $r_d \approx 379.2\ \mathrm{mm}$ (assumed $0.98$).

**Exam tip:** Write the code breakdown first, convert inches to mm, then show each radius on its own line with units. Common slip: forgetting to double the sidewall height for the diameter, or mixing inches and mm.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-3d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the largest force you can apply at the shoe tip (expander force $F_e$) before the wheel locks, written as a multiple of the wheel normal load $W$. Tyre grip limits the wheel torque to $\mu_a W R$; divide by the drum radius to get the drum drag, then divide by the shoe factor: $F_e = \mu_a W R/(r S)$.

**Given:**
- $R = 400\ \mathrm{mm}$ (tyre radius)
- $r = 125\ \mathrm{mm}$ (drum radius)
- $\mu_a = 0.8$ (tyre-road)
- $\mu = 0.3$ (shoe-drum)
- $S = 5$ (shoe factor)

**To find:**
- maximum braking force at the shoe tip $F_e$ as a function of the wheel normal load $W$

1. Symbols: $W$ normal load on the wheel, $R=0.400$ m tyre radius, $r=0.125$ m drum radius, $\mu_a=0.8$ tyre-road, $\mu=0.3$ lining-drum, $S=5$ shoe factor, $F_e$ shoe-tip (expander) force.
2. Sketch the drum (radius $r$) inside the wheel (tyre radius $R$) with the rotation arrow; on the shoe mark the tip (expander) force $F_e$, the radial lining force $N$ and the tangential drag $F_t=\mu N$ on the drum; at the road mark $W$ and the braking force $\mu_a W$.
3. Maximum wheel braking torque is set by adhesion at incipient skid.
$$T_b=\mu_a W R=0.8\,W\,(0.4)=0.32\,W\ \mathrm{N\,m}$$
4. Tangential drum drag needed to supply that torque.
$$F_t=\frac{T_b}{r}=\frac{0.32\,W}{0.125}=2.56\,W$$
5. Shoe factor is tangential drag divided by shoe-tip force, $S=F_t/F_e$, so the shoe-tip force is
$$F_e=\frac{F_t}{S}=\frac{2.56\,W}{5}=0.512\,W$$
6. Check: the radial lining force is $N=F_t/\mu=2.56W/0.3=8.53\,W$. The factor $\mu$ is not used again in $F_e$ because $S$ already contains it.

**Answer:** Maximum shoe-tip force $F_e = 0.512\,W$ ($W$ in newtons). Required drum drag $F_t = 2.56\,W$, lining normal force $N = 8.53\,W$.

**Exam tip:** Write the formula first, substitute the given values on one line, then box the final force. Common slip: mixing the wheel radius with the drum radius.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-3e'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants four sketches of tread patterns, one each for a commercial vehicle, an off-roader, a tractor and a winter tyre, with a note on why each looks different.

1. General rule: circumferential ribs give lateral grip and steering guidance; transverse bars and lugs give traction and braking grip; grooves drain water; sipes add biting edges.
2. Commercial vehicle: draw circumferential zig-zag ribs with drainage grooves for a steer wheel, and stronger transverse or diagonal bars and blocks for a drive wheel.
3. Off-roader (off/on-road): bold separated blocks with wide open grooves so the tyre grips loose ground but still runs on tarmac.
4. Tractor: tall diagonal bars (lugs) in a chevron or herringbone, open centre, the chevron pointing in the direction of rotation; the lugs dig into soil for drawbar pull and the open gaps self-clean mud.
5. Winter tyre: many blocks cut by fine slits (sipes) giving biting edges on snow and ice, wide grooves to clear slush, and a softer compound that stays flexible in the cold.
6. Label ribs, grooves, blocks, sipes or lugs on each and write one line on why it suits that duty.

**Answer:** Four labelled tread sketches (commercial, off-roader, tractor, winter), each with a one-line reason.

**Exam tip:** Draw four small, clearly different tread sketches and write one duty line under each; about one page. Common slip: sketches that all look alike with no label.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-4a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a list of the warning and indicator lamps and gauges on a car dashboard, with what each tells the driver.

1. Start with the definition: a warning lamp reports a fault or unsafe condition, an indicator reports a normal operating state.
2. Safety and warning group: oil pressure lamp, coolant temperature (overheat) lamp, battery/charging lamp, brake warning lamp (low fluid or parking brake applied), ABS lamp, seat belt lamp, door-ajar lamp, airbag lamp, engine malfunction (check engine) lamp.
3. Operation group: fuel level gauge and low-fuel lamp, speedometer and odometer, tachometer, direction indicator (turn signal) arrows, hazard flasher, high-beam lamp, parking/headlamp-on lamp.
4. One-line function for each (needed for the 3-mark version): oil lamp = lubrication pressure low; temperature lamp = engine overheating; battery lamp = charging system fault, not a charge gauge; brake lamp = fluid low or parking brake on; ABS lamp = anti-lock system fault; belt lamp = seat belt not fastened; fuel lamp = low fuel; turn arrows = signal in use; high-beam lamp = main beam on (not a fault).
5. Many lamps glow for a moment at ignition-on as a bulb self-check; a lamp that stays on after the engine starts is the warning.

**Answer:** Dashboard indicators: oil pressure, coolant temperature, battery/charging, brake, ABS, seat belt, door ajar, airbag, engine malfunction, fuel level/low fuel, speedometer, tachometer, turn signals, hazard, high beam.

**Exam tip:** Make two short columns (safety/warning and operation) with 8 to 10 items and a 3 to 5 word function against each; the common slip is writing names only, or calling the battery lamp a charge gauge.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-4b'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a list of the harmful gases and particles that come out of the exhaust pipe (CO, HC, NOx, particulates and so on) with the cause of each.

1. Tail-pipe emissions are what leaves the exhaust pipe after combustion. List them with their cause.
2. Carbon monoxide (CO): incomplete combustion, rich mixture.
3. Unburned hydrocarbons (HC) and other organic gases: fuel that did not burn (misfire, quench layers, rich mixture).
4. Oxides of nitrogen ($\mathrm{NO_x}$): at high combustion temperature, $\mathrm{N_2}$ and $\mathrm{O_2}$ from the air combine. $\mathrm{NO_x}$ peaks near stoichiometric to slightly lean (about $5$ to $10$ percent excess air), where temperature is highest and spare oxygen is present. Rich and very lean mixtures give less.
5. Particulate matter (PM, soot): mainly diesel engines and gasoline direct injection.
6. Carbon dioxide ($\mathrm{CO_2}$): complete combustion product; not toxic but a greenhouse gas. Water vapour and nitrogen are the bulk harmless products.
7. Sulphur dioxide ($\mathrm{SO_2}$) from sulphur in fuel can be added as a minor item. Ozone is not a direct exhaust product (it forms in the atmosphere); fuel-tank evaporation and crankcase blow-by are vehicle emissions but not tail-pipe ones.

**Answer:** Tail-pipe emissions: CO, unburned HC, $\mathrm{NO_x}$, particulate matter, $\mathrm{CO_2}$ (plus minor $\mathrm{SO_2}$ and water vapour).

**Exam tip:** List CO, HC, $\mathrm{NO_x}$, PM and $\mathrm{CO_2}$ with the cause of each in one line; for 2 marks five names with a short cause is enough. Common slip: adding ozone or fuel-tank vapour, which are not tail-pipe emissions.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-4c'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a short description of how a hybrid vehicle drives the wheels using both an engine and an electric motor, with the main layouts named.

1. Definition: a hybrid electric vehicle combines an internal combustion engine with one or more electric machines and an energy store (battery), so each is used where it is most efficient.
2. Power drive in a series hybrid: engine drives a generator, electricity goes to the battery or to the traction motor, and only the traction motor turns the wheels. The engine is not mechanically connected to the wheels.
3. Power drive in a parallel hybrid: engine and electric motor both have mechanical paths to the wheels through a coupling and the transmission, and can drive together or separately.
4. Regenerative braking: while slowing, the motor acts as a generator, converting part of the vehicle kinetic energy to electricity stored in the battery.
5. Benefits: better fuel economy and lower emissions, engine run near its efficient point, stop-start and electric-only low-speed running.

**Answer:** A hybrid drive pairs an engine with an electric motor and battery; in series only the motor drives the wheels, in parallel both drive them, and regenerative braking recharges the battery.

**Exam tip:** Draw block diagrams with arrows (engine, generator, battery, motor, wheels) and label power flow; 3 to 5 marks want definition plus series and parallel in two or three lines each plus regen braking. Common slip: forgetting that in series the engine has no mechanical link to the wheels.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-4d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a labelled sketch of a single plate dry clutch (flywheel, friction plate, pressure plate, springs, release lever, bearing) and a short working description.

1. Purpose line: a friction clutch connects and disconnects the engine from the gearbox, allows smooth take-up from rest and a break in drive for gear changes.
2. Draw the engine crankshaft ending in the flywheel as a large vertical disc; the flywheel is bolted to the crank flange.
3. Bolt the clutch cover to the flywheel rim. Inside the cover draw the pressure plate facing the flywheel and the spring that loads it (the diaphragm spring in the lab drawing, or a ring of coil springs).
4. Between the flywheel and pressure plate draw the driven plate (clutch disc): a thin steel disc with a friction lining on each face (two faces, hence two friction surfaces), cushion segments between the linings, and a central hub fitted with torsional damper springs.
5. Splined hub: draw the hub sliding on the splined gearbox input (clutch) shaft, whose spigot end runs in a pilot bush in the crankshaft.
6. Release mechanism: release (throw-out) bearing on a sleeve over the input shaft acting on the diaphragm fingers or release levers, operated by the fork and the pedal linkage or hydraulic cylinder. In the lab drawing (pull type) the fingers are pulled to release.
7. Show the engaged torque path with arrows: engine, flywheel and cover, pressure plate, friction linings, driven plate hub, splined shaft to gearbox. Add a second small arrow for release motion.
8. Working in two lines: pedal released, spring clamps the disc between flywheel and pressure plate and torque is transmitted by friction; pedal pressed, the pressure plate is withdrawn, the clamping load goes and the engine turns without driving the gearbox.
9. Closing: torque capacity is limited by friction, $T = z\mu W R_m$, where z = number of friction surfaces, mu = coefficient of friction, W = total axial spring force on the plate (N), R_m = mean friction radius (m). For uniform wear (worn-in clutch, the usual design assumption) $R_m=\frac{r_1+r_2}{2}$. For uniform pressure (new clutch) $R_m=\frac{2}{3}\,\frac{r_1^3-r_2^3}{r_1^2-r_2^2}$, where $r_1$ and $r_2$ are the outer and inner lining radii.
$$T=z\,\mu\,W\,R_m,\quad z=2$$
10. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Multi-plate clutch.** Several friction discs on a splined hub alternate with steel plates, all pressed by springs. Torque capacity $T = n\,\mu\,W\,R_m$ where $n$ is the number of friction surfaces ($n = n_1 + n_2 - 1$). Compact for a given torque, so used in motorcycles, heavy vehicles and automatic gearboxes; the wet type runs in an oil bath.
11. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Centrifugal clutch.** Shoes or weights pivoted on the driving member fly outwards above a set engine speed and grip the drum, so it engages automatically with no pedal. Used in scooters and mopeds; the semi-centrifugal type adds a spring for extra pressure at low speed.
12. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Clutch failure symptoms.** Slip: engine rpm rises but vehicle speed does not (worn lining, weak spring, oil on facing, no free play). Drag or spin: clutch does not release fully, gears grind (excess free play, warped plate, air in hydraulic line). Judder: jerky take-off (uneven lining, loose mounting). Noise: worn release bearing.

**Answer:** Single plate dry clutch: flywheel, driven plate with two linings on a splined hub, pressure plate, spring, cover and release bearing; spring clamp transmits torque, pedal removes the clamp.

**Exam tip:** Draw a clean sectional half-view in the order flywheel, friction disc, pressure plate, spring, cover, release bearing and fork, input shaft, and label every part; add two lines on working. Common slip: one friction face instead of two, or missing the splined hub.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-4e'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a sketch tracing the route of power from the engine to the driven rear wheels (clutch, gearbox, propeller shaft, final drive, differential, axle shafts).

1. Layout of a rear-wheel-drive vehicle: engine at the front, drive taken backwards to the rear axle.
2. Draw the engine and flywheel at the front, the clutch directly behind it, then the gearbox, in one line along the vehicle.
3. From the gearbox output shaft draw the propeller shaft with a universal joint at each end (and a slip joint to allow length change).
4. At the rear draw the final drive: pinion and crown wheel, the differential cage and its gears, then the two half (axle) shafts to the rear wheels.
5. Power flow in words: engine crankshaft, flywheel, clutch, gearbox (gear ratio selected), propeller shaft with U-joints, final drive pinion and crown wheel (right-angle turn and speed reduction), differential, half shafts, rear wheels.
6. Note that the gearbox output speed is not the wheel speed: the final drive reduces speed again and the differential lets the wheels turn at different speeds on a bend.
7. Label: engine, flywheel, clutch, gearbox, propeller shaft, universal joints, final drive, differential, rear axle shaft, wheel.
8. Also asked as 2024 Q5(b) (2 marks): represent the layout of the propeller shaft schematically for a rear wheel drive vehicle. Write: the axle moves up and down with the suspension while engine and gearbox are fixed, so the propeller shaft must change both angle (two universal joints) and length (slip joint).
9. From the gearbox output shaft draw the first universal joint, then the propeller shaft, then the second universal joint at the rear.
10. Draw a splined slip joint (sliding sleeve) at one end of the propeller shaft: it takes up the change in length as the axle moves.
11. Connect the second joint to the pinion shaft of the final drive and differential on the rear axle, with the half shafts and wheels.
12. Show the axle moving up and down by a dotted outline to explain the angle change. For a long shaft add a two-piece shaft with a centre bearing.
13. Label: engine, clutch, gearbox, UJ1, slip joint, propeller shaft, UJ2, final drive/differential, half shafts, rear wheels.
14. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Front-wheel drive layout.** Engine, clutch, gearbox and final drive form one compact unit at the front; drive goes by half shafts with CV joints. No propeller shaft, so more floor space and good traction on slippery roads, but steering and driving loads act on the same wheels.
15. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Four-wheel (all-wheel) drive.** A transfer case splits power from the gearbox to front and rear propeller shafts, each with its own differential; used for off-road and traction. A centre differential or viscous coupling lets front and rear axles turn at different speeds.
16. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Propeller shaft details.** Tubular shaft with a universal joint at each end and a slip joint (splined) to allow length change as the rear axle moves with the suspension; it may be balanced and supported by a centre bearing when long.

**Answer:** Engine, flywheel, clutch, gearbox, propeller shaft with universal joints, final drive and differential, half shafts, rear wheels.

**Exam tip:** Draw a side-view block layout with parts in order and arrows for power flow, and label every box; write one line of function per part. Common slip: swapping clutch and gearbox, or leaving out the universal joints and differential.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-5a'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants three one-line definitions: a universal joint, a constant velocity (CV) joint and a ball joint, with where each is used.

1. **Universal joint (Hooke''s joint):** two yokes joined by a cross (spider) with four needle-bearing trunnions; it transmits torque between two shafts whose axes meet at an angle that can change.
2. Its output speed fluctuates twice per revolution when the shaft angle is not zero, with extremes $N_{in}\cos\beta$ and $N_{in}/\cos\beta$. Two correctly phased joints with equal angles cancel the fluctuation.
3. **CV joint (constant-velocity, e.g. Rzeppa ball type):** balls running in matching grooves of an inner and an outer race, held in the plane that bisects the shaft angle, so the driven shaft turns at exactly the same speed as the driving shaft at every instant. Used on front-wheel-drive half shafts where the angle is large and changes.
4. **Ball joint:** a spherical pin (ball stud) in a socket that allows angular movement in all directions while carrying linkage force. It is a steering/suspension joint, not a shaft-drive joint.

**Answer:** Universal joint: transmits torque across a varying angle with cyclic speed variation. CV joint: transmits torque across an angle with constant output speed. Ball joint: spherical pin and socket for angular freedom in suspension and steering links.

**Exam tip:** Give each joint 3 to 4 lines plus a tiny sketch (cross and two yokes; balls in grooves; ball in socket). Slip: describing the ball joint as a drive joint.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-5b'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a sketch of the H-pattern of the gear lever (positions of 1st to 5th and reverse) used in manual cars, not a calculation.

1. Draw the H: two vertical gate lines joined by a horizontal neutral bar across the middle.
2. Left gate: 1 at the top, 2 at the bottom. Middle gate: 3 at the top, 4 at the bottom. Right gate: 5 at the top, R at the bottom.
3. Mark the neutral line (N) joining the gates; the lever passes through neutral when moving sideways, which moves the selector from one fork to the next.
4. State that this is the common five-speed H-pattern; reverse position and lockout vary between makes.

**Answer:** H-pattern: 1-2 left gate, 3-4 middle gate, 5-R right gate, neutral bar across the middle.

**Exam tip:** Draw a clear H with 1-2, 3-4, 5-R and label the neutral line. For 2 marks the drawing plus 1 to 2 lines is enough. Slip: placing 2 above 1 or leaving out neutral.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-5c'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the approximate acceleration at 100 km/h and at 60 km/h. Use $F = P/v$ for the tractive force at each speed, then $a = F/m$. The paper gives power only at top speed, so you assume the power at 60 km/h yourself.

**Given:**
- $P = 100\ \mathrm{kW}$
- $m = 2000\ \mathrm{kg}$
- $V_1 = 100\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $V_2 = 60\ \mathrm{km/h}$

**To find:**
- (i) tentative acceleration $a_1$ at 100 km/h
- (ii) tentative acceleration $a_2$ at 60 km/h

1. Convert speeds.
$$v=\frac{V}{3.6}\ \Rightarrow\ v_1=\frac{100}{3.6}=27.78\ \mathrm{m/s},\quad v_2=\frac{60}{3.6}=16.67\ \mathrm{m/s}$$
2. (i) Tractive force at 100 km/h.
$$F_1 = \frac{P}{v_1} = \frac{100000}{27.78} = 3600\ \mathrm{N}$$
3. (i) Acceleration at 100 km/h.
$$a_1 = \frac{F_1}{m} = \frac{3600}{2000} = 1.80\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$$
4. (ii) Assumption for 60 km/h: a lower gear lets the engine run at full-power speed, so 100 kW is again available at the wheels.
$$F_2=\frac{P}{v_2}=\frac{100000}{16.67}=6000\ \mathrm{N},\quad a_2=\frac{F_2}{m}=\frac{6000}{2000}=3.00\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$$
5. (ii) If instead only 60 kW were available at 60 km/h, the result would be $a_2 = 60000/(2000\times16.67) = 1.80\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$.

**Answer:** (i) $a_1 = 1.80\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$ at 100 km/h; (ii) $a_2 = 3.00\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$ at 60 km/h (assuming 100 kW is available there).

**Exam tip:** Write the formula $a = P/(mv)$ first, convert units to m/s and W, then box each answer with unit. Slip: using km/h directly or forgetting kW to W.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-5d'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a graph (engine torque or tractive effort on the vertical axis against vehicle speed on the horizontal) with one curve for each gear, showing how lower gears give more torque at low speed.

1. Draw axes: vehicle speed $v$ (km/h) on the horizontal axis and tractive force $F$ (N) on the vertical axis.
2. For each gear ratio $i$ convert an engine point $(N, T)$ to a graph point.
$$v = \frac{2\pi r N}{60\, i},\qquad F = \frac{\eta\, T\, i}{r}$$
3. Plot one curve per gear. Each curve has the shape of the engine torque curve (it rises to a peak at the maximum-torque rpm, then falls), scaled up in force by $i$ and squeezed in speed by $1/i$. First gear is highest in force but ends at the lowest speed; top gear is lowest in force and reaches the highest speed. Curves of adjacent gears overlap, so the best gear at a speed is the upper curve.
4. Draw the road-resistance curve on the same axes (rolling resistance plus a rising aerodynamic term, plus gradient term if any).
5. Mark the intersection of the top-gear curve with the resistance curve: this is the maximum speed. The vertical gap between a gear curve and the resistance curve is the surplus force available to accelerate or climb.
6. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Tractive effort versus speed.** $F_t = \dfrac{T\,i_g\,i_o\,\eta}{r}$ and road speed $v = 0.377\,\dfrac{N\,r}{i_g\,i_o}$ (km/h, $r$ in m). Plotting $F_t$ against $v$ for each gear gives a set of curves under the ideal constant-power hyperbola; the road-resistance curve crosses them at the maximum speed in each gear.
7. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Gradeability and acceleration reserve.** Tractive effort left after road resistance is the reserve: $\sin\theta \approx \dfrac{F_t - R_r - R_a}{W}$ for gradeability. Lowest gear gives the largest $F_t$, so it sets the gradeability; the top gear sets the maximum speed.
8. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Ideal gear-ratio selection.** First gear from gradeability or starting, top gear from maximum speed; the intermediate ratios are chosen in geometric progression $\dfrac{i_1}{i_2} = \dfrac{i_2}{i_3} = \dots = K$, $K = \left(\dfrac{i_1}{i_n}\right)^{\frac{1}{n-1}}$, so engine speed range is the same at each shift.

**Answer:** A family of humped tractive-force (wheel torque) curves, one per gear, each copying the engine torque curve: low gear high and short, top gear low and long. The road-resistance curve is drawn on the same axes; its intersection with the top-gear curve is the top speed and the vertical gap is the surplus force.

**Exam tip:** Label both axes with units, draw 4 to 5 gear curves and the resistance curve, mark the top-speed intersection. Slip: curves that rise with speed or a resistance line that is straight.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = '23-5e'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the ratio of each of 4 forward gears plus the final drive. Rolling resistance plus gradient resistance only (no air drag) set the bottom gear; the max speed 180 km/h at 3500 rpm with 400 mm tyre sets the top gear and final drive; intermediate gears follow a geometric progression.

**Given:**
- $m = 3000\ \mathrm{kg}$
- $V_{max} = 180\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $N_{max} = 3500\ \mathrm{rpm}$
- $r = 400\ \mathrm{mm}$
- $T_{peak} = 250\ \mathrm{N\,m}$
- 4 forward gears

**To find:**
- (i) final drive ratio $i_f$
- (ii) gearbox ratios $i_1, i_2, i_3, i_4$

1. (i) Top speed in m/s.
$$v_{max}=\frac{V}{3.6}=\frac{180}{3.6}=50\ \mathrm{m/s}$$
2. (i) Final drive from direct top gear at maximum rpm (top gear ratio 1:1).
$$i_f = \frac{2\pi r N}{60\, v_{max}} = \frac{2\pi(0.40)(3500)}{60(50)} = 2.9322$$
3. (ii) Maximum road load at low speed (rolling and gradient only, aerodynamic drag neglected), grade 1 in 5.
$$\theta=\tan^{-1}G=\tan^{-1}(0.2)=11.310^\circ,\quad F=mg(\sin\theta+C_r\cos\theta)=3000(9.81)(0.1961+0.015\times0.9806)=6204.6\ \mathrm{N}$$
4. (ii) First gear from tractive force at the wheels.
$$i_1 = \frac{F\, r}{T\, i_f\, \eta} = \frac{6204.6(0.40)}{250(2.9322)(0.85)} = 3.9831$$
5. (ii) Geometric progression with $i_4 = 1$: three steps from first to fourth.
$$K = \left(\frac{i_4}{i_1}\right)^{1/3} = 0.6308,\quad i_2 = i_1K = 2.5128,\quad i_3 = i_1K^2 = 1.5852$$
6. Check: top gear at 3500 rpm must recover 180 km/h, and $i_1 i_f \eta T / r$ must recover the road load.
7. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Types of gearbox.** Sliding mesh: gears slide to mesh, needs double declutching, noisy. Constant mesh: gears always in mesh, dog clutches engage them. Synchromesh: cones equalise speeds before dogs engage, so shifts are smooth and quiet; used in most cars.
8. Added (related topic, may be asked): **Geometric progression of ratios.** Once first and top ratios are fixed, the intermediate ratios follow $K = \left(\dfrac{i_n}{i_1}\right)^{\frac{1}{n-1}}$ (less than 1), $i_2 = i_1K$, $i_3 = i_1K^2$, so the engine speed drop at every shift is equal.
9. Added (related topic, may be asked): **What fixes each ratio.** Final drive ratio comes from maximum speed in top gear: $i_o = 0.377\,\dfrac{N\,r}{v_{max}\,i_{top}}$; the lowest gear ratio comes from the maximum gradient to be climbed at peak torque, with a check that the wheels do not slip.

**Answer:** (i) final drive $i_f = 2.93:1$; (ii) gearbox ratios $i_1 = 3.98$, $i_2 = 2.51$, $i_3 = 1.59$, $i_4 = 1.00$ (overall in first $= 11.68$).

**Exam tip:** Show the four steps in order with the formulas and a final table of ratios. Slip: using the overall ratio of top gear as the gearbox ratio, or leaving out the efficiency or gradient term.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = 'm24a-1'), 1, '**What it asks:** Find the front and rear axle weights (FAW, RAW) in three situations: standing still, climbing a 6% slope while accelerating at 6 ft/s$^2$, and also the steepest slope the car could climb (limit when the front axle load goes to zero or traction runs out).

**Given:**
- $W = 3246\ \mathrm{lb}$
- $h = 20\ \mathrm{in}$
- $a = 40\ \mathrm{in}$ (CG from front wheel)
- $l = 106\ \mathrm{in}$
- gradient $6\%$, $\tan\theta = 0.06$
- $a_x = 6\ \mathrm{ft/s^2}$

**To find:**
- (i) FAW and RAW under static conditions
- (ii) FAW and RAW climbing a $6\%$ gradient while accelerating at $6\ \mathrm{ft/s^2}$
- (iii) maximum gradient the car can climb

1. Data.
$$W=3246\ \mathrm{lb},\ l=106,\ a=40,\ b=l-a=66,\ h=20\ \mathrm{in}$$
2. (i) (a) Static.
$$\mathrm{FAW}=\frac{Wb}{l}=\frac{3246\times66}{106}=2021\ \mathrm{lb},\quad \mathrm{RAW}=\frac{Wa}{l}=\frac{3246\times40}{106}=1225\ \mathrm{lb}$$
3. (ii) (b) 6% grade accelerating at $6\ \mathrm{ft/s^2}$.
$$\theta=\tan^{-1}0.06,\ \cos\theta=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+\tan^2\theta}}=0.9982,\ \sin\theta=0.0599,\ \frac{a_x}{g}h=\frac{6}{32.2}\times20=3.73\ \mathrm{in}$$
4. (ii) Axle loads.
$$\mathrm{FAW}=\frac{W(b\cos\theta-h\sin\theta-\frac{a_x}{g}h)}{l}=\frac{3246(65.88-1.20-3.73)}{106}=1867\ \mathrm{lb}$$
5. (ii) Rear axle.
$$\mathrm{RAW}=\frac{W(a\cos\theta+h\sin\theta+\frac{a_x}{g}h)}{l}=\frac{3246(39.93+1.20+3.73)}{106}=1374\ \mathrm{lb}$$
6. (ii) Check.
$$\mathrm{FAW}+\mathrm{RAW}=W\cos\theta:\ 1867+1374=3241\approx3240\ \mathrm{lb}$$
7. (iii) Maximum gradient from the data given: the front axle just unloads ($\mathrm{FAW} = 0$), the car is about to tip back.
$$b\cos\theta=h\sin\theta\ \Rightarrow\ \tan\theta=\frac{b}{h}=\frac{66}{20}=3.3\ \Rightarrow\ \theta=73.1^\circ$$
8. (iii) If the $6\ \mathrm{ft/s^2}$ acceleration is also kept, the same condition $b\cos\theta - h\sin\theta = (a_x/g)\,h$ gives 70.0 degrees (tan = 2.75). In practice tyre grip limits the car long before this.
9. (iii) Traction-limited gradient (the one a car actually meets), assuming $\mu = 0.8$ at steady speed.
$$\tan\theta_{RWD}=\frac{0.8\times40}{106-0.8\times20}=0.356,\quad \tan\theta_{FWD}=\frac{0.8\times66}{106+0.8\times20}=0.433$$

**Answer:** (i) Static: $\mathrm{FAW} = 2021\ \mathrm{lb}$, $\mathrm{RAW} = 1225\ \mathrm{lb}$. (ii) Climbing $6\%$ at $6\ \mathrm{ft/s^2}$: $\mathrm{FAW} = 1867\ \mathrm{lb}$, $\mathrm{RAW} = 1374\ \mathrm{lb}$. (iii) Maximum gradient: no friction is given, so write the traction limit as a formula and quote a value: RWD $\tan\theta = \mu a/(l - \mu h)$, FWD $\tan\theta = \mu b/(l + \mu h)$; with $\mu = 0.8$ that is $0.356$ ($35.6\%$, $19.6^\circ$) for RWD and $0.433$ ($43.3\%$, $23.4^\circ$) for FWD. The tip-back limit (front axle unloads) is far higher, $\tan\theta = 3.3$ ($73.1^\circ$), $70.0^\circ$ if the $6\ \mathrm{ft/s^2}$ is kept.

**Exam tip:** Show data, static loads, then slope and acceleration terms separately, and state clearly the $\mu$ you assumed for the gradient. Slip: mixing inches and feet.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = 'm24a-2'), 1, '**What it asks:** For each of the two road profiles, convert 50 mph to ft/s, find the bump frequency $f = \nu v$, then the peak acceleration of the wheel, $a = A(2\pi f)^2$. ''nu'' (the Greek letter) is bumps per foot, not a frequency in Hz.

**Given:**
- $z = A\sin(2\pi\nu x)$
- $V = 50\ \mathrm{mph}$
- (i) $A = 10\ \mathrm{in}$, $\nu = 0.005\ \mathrm{cycle/ft}$
- (ii) $A = 0.01\ \mathrm{in}$, $\nu = 0.5\ \mathrm{cycle/ft}$

**To find:**
- (i) frequency $f$ and acceleration amplitude for $A = 10\ \mathrm{in}$, $\nu = 0.005\ \mathrm{cycle/ft}$
- (ii) frequency $f$ and acceleration amplitude for $A = 0.01\ \mathrm{in}$, $\nu = 0.5\ \mathrm{cycle/ft}$

1. Speed.
$$v=\frac{V\times5280}{3600}=\frac{50\times5280}{3600}=73.33\ \mathrm{ft/s}$$
2. (i) (i) Frequency.
$$f=v\nu=73.33\times0.005=0.367\ \mathrm{Hz}$$
3. (i) (i) Acceleration amplitude with A = 10/12 ft.
$$\ddot z=A(2\pi f)^2=\frac{10}{12}(2\pi\times0.367)^2=4.42\ \mathrm{ft/s^2}=0.137\,g$$
4. (ii) (ii) Frequency.
$$f=v\nu=73.33\times0.5=36.67\ \mathrm{Hz}$$
5. (ii) (ii) Acceleration amplitude with A = 0.01/12 ft.
$$\ddot z=A(2\pi f)^2=\frac{0.01}{12}(2\pi\times36.67)^2=44.2\ \mathrm{ft/s^2}=1.37\,g$$
6. Lesson: acceleration grows with f squared, so a tiny short-wavelength roughness shakes ten times harder than a large slow undulation.

**Answer:** (i) $f = 0.367\ \mathrm{Hz}$, acceleration amplitude $4.42\ \mathrm{ft/s^2}$ ($0.137g$). (ii) $f = 36.7\ \mathrm{Hz}$, acceleration amplitude $44.2\ \mathrm{ft/s^2}$ ($1.37g$).

**Exam tip:** Convert units first, then $f = v\nu$, then $A(2\pi f)^2$, and quote the answer also in g. Slip: using inches for A when speed is in feet.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = 'm24a-3'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants four line diagrams, one each for leaf spring, double wishbone, MacPherson strut and semi-trailing suspension, with the roll centre located on each.

1. Leaf spring (rigid axle): RC roughly at spring-eye or spring-seat height on the centreline, as in 2024 Q2(e)a.
2. Double wishbone: extend both arms to $I$, join to contact point, repeat, crossing is RC (2023 Q2(e)a).
3. MacPherson strut: perpendicular to strut at the top mount meets the extended lower arm at $I$; join to contact point on both sides (2023 Q2(e)b).
4. Semi-trailing arm: virtual pivot from the oblique axis, join to contact points (2024 Q2(e)c). Label RC on every sketch.

**Answer:** Four line diagrams with the roll centre located by the instant-centre construction (leaf spring: at spring height).

**Exam tip:** Draw four small, labelled sketches, each with construction lines and RC marked on the centreline. About a quarter page each is enough; the usual slip is drawing only two of the four.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = 'm24b-1'), 1, '**What it asks:** It is a bundle of tyre definitions: dynamic radius, slip angle, contact patch length, and the three stiffness values (radial, longitudinal, lateral). Give each a one or two line definition, with formula where one exists.

1. Dynamic radius: $r_{dyn}=V/\omega$ for a freely rolling wheel; see 24-3c.
2. Slip angle $\alpha$: angle between the wheel plane and the direction of travel, $\tan\alpha=V_y/V_x$; see 24-3a.
3. Contact-patch length: for a small radial deflection $\delta$ of a tyre of free radius $r$ the chord on the ground is
$$L\approx 2\sqrt{2r\delta}$$
4. Radial (vertical) stiffness: vertical load per unit radial deflection.
$$k_z=\frac{F_z}{\delta}\ \ (\text{or } dF_z/d\delta)$$
5. Longitudinal stiffness: fore-aft force per unit fore-aft deflection of the tread relative to the rim, $k_x=F_x/\Delta x$; the related slip stiffness is the slope at zero slip.
$$k_x=\frac{F_x}{\Delta x},\quad C_s=\frac{dF_x}{ds}\Big|_{s=0}$$
6. Lateral stiffness: side force per unit sideways deflection, $k_y=F_y/\Delta y$; the related cornering stiffness is the slope against slip angle at zero angle.
$$k_y=\frac{F_y}{\Delta y},\quad C_\alpha=\frac{dF_y}{d\alpha}\Big|_{\alpha=0}$$

**Answer:** $r_{dyn}=V/\omega$; $\tan\alpha=V_y/V_x$; patch length $L\approx 2\sqrt{2r\delta}$; stiffness = force per deflection: $k_z=F_z/\delta$, $k_x=F_x/\Delta x$, $k_y=F_y/\Delta y$ (slip forms $C_s=dF_x/ds$, $C_\alpha=dF_y/d\alpha$).

**Exam tip:** One short line per item, each with its formula and a labelled sketch where you can. Common slip: giving definitions without formulas.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = 'm24b-2'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants a derivation (a formula built step by step) of $a$ that includes both the mass being pushed forward and the energy needed to spin the engine and wheels faster. The result has the form $a = F/(\delta m)$ where $\delta$ is the mass factor.

1. Rotating parts: engine and flywheel inertia $I_e$ spinning at $\omega_e = i\, v/r$ (overall ratio $i$), wheels and axle inertia $I_w$ at $\omega_w = v/r$.
2. Total kinetic energy.
$$E = \tfrac12 m v^2 + \tfrac12 I_e \omega_e^2 + \tfrac12 I_w \omega_w^2 = \tfrac12\left[m + \frac{I_e i^2 + I_w}{r^2}\right]v^2$$
3. Equivalent mass and mass factor.
$$m_{eq} = \delta\, m,\qquad \delta = 1 + \frac{I_e\, i^2 + I_w}{m\, r^2}$$
4. Work-energy: the net tractive force does work $(F - R)\,ds = d E$, so differentiating with $v = ds/dt$ gives the equation of motion.
$$(F - R) = \delta\, m\, a \;\Rightarrow\; a = \frac{F - R}{\delta\, m}$$
5. Tractive force from the engine.
$$F = \frac{\eta\, T\, i}{r},\qquad a = \frac{\eta T i / r - R}{\delta\, m}$$
6. $\delta$ is larger in low gear (large $i$) because the engine inertia is multiplied by $i^2$; with $\delta = 1$ the expression reduces to the tentative acceleration.

**Answer:** $a = (F - R)/(\delta m)$, with $\delta = 1 + (I_e i^2 + I_w)/(m r^2)$, $F = \eta T i / r$.

**Exam tip:** Write kinetic energy of the three parts, define $\delta$, apply work-energy and state the final formula. Slip: forgetting the $i^2$ on the engine inertia.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = 'm25-1'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants the acceleration in 1st and in 4th gear: find tractive force $F = T i / r$ for each gear, then $a = F/(\delta m)$ with that gear''s mass factor (1.50 for 1st, 1.11 for 4th). Read 4.2 as the first-gear gearbox ratio and 2.92 as the final drive (fourth gear direct), so $i_1 = 4.2\times2.92 = 12.264$ and $i_4 = 2.92$. This reading gives about 1630 rpm at 20 km/h in first and 1160 rpm at 60 km/h in fourth, which is realistic.

**Given:**
- $m = 3\ \mathrm{t}$
- $T = 247\ \mathrm{N\,m}$
- $\delta_1 = 1.50,\ \delta_4 = 1.11$
- $r = 400\ \mathrm{mm}$
- $V = 20,\ 60\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $i_{g1} = 4.2,\ i_f = 2.92$

**To find:**
- (i) acceleration $a_1$ in first gear
- (ii) acceleration $a_4$ in fourth gear

1. Assumed reading (the paper copy does not state it): first-gear ratio 4.2, final drive 2.92, fourth gear direct (1:1). Overall ratios:
$$i=i_g\times i_f:\quad i_1=4.2\times 2.92=12.264,\qquad i_4=1\times 2.92=2.92$$
2. Tractive force at the wheel, $F = T i / r$ ($r = 0.4\ \mathrm{m}$, $\eta = 1$).
$$F=\frac{T\,i}{r}:\quad F_1=\frac{247\times 12.264}{0.4}=7573\ \mathrm{N},\qquad F_4=\frac{247\times 2.92}{0.4}=1803\ \mathrm{N}$$
3. Acceleration with the mass factor (rotating parts), m = 3000 kg.
$$a=\frac{F}{\delta\,m}:\quad a_1=\frac{7573}{1.50\times 3000}=1.683\ \mathrm{m/s^2},\qquad a_4=\frac{1803}{1.11\times 3000}=0.541\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$$
4. Why first gear accelerates much harder: the overall ratio is 4.2 times bigger, even though the mass factor is larger.

**Answer:** (i) $a_1 = 1.68\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$ in first gear (overall ratio $4.2 \times 2.92 = 12.264$); (ii) $a_4 = 0.541\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$ in fourth gear (direct, overall $2.92$).

**Exam tip:** Show ratio, force, $\delta$ and acceleration for each gear in a small table. Slip: using the first-gear $\delta = 1.50$ for fourth gear.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = 'm25-2'), 1, '**What it asks:** Find the slip angle of the front tyres and of the rear tyres in a steady turn, then compare them: if front slip is larger the car understeers, if rear is larger it oversteers. Work out the axle loads from the CG position, the lateral force from $mv^2/R$, and divide by the cornering stiffness.

**Given:**
- $m = 3000\ \mathrm{kg}$
- $L = 2.1\ \mathrm{m}$ (wheelbase)
- $a = 0.9\ \mathrm{m}$ (CG behind front axle)
- $R = 25\ \mathrm{m}$
- $V = 60\ \mathrm{km/h}$
- $C_\alpha = 150\ \mathrm{kgf/deg}$ (two tyres of an axle combined)

**To find:**
- (i) front tyre slip angle $\alpha_f$
- (ii) rear tyre slip angle $\alpha_r$
- (iii) whether the vehicle oversteers or understeers

1. Speed and lateral acceleration:
$$v=\frac{V}{3.6}=\frac{60}{3.6}=16.67\ \mathrm{m/s},\quad a_y=\frac{v^2}{R}=\frac{16.67^2}{25}=11.11\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$$
2. Total lateral force:
$$F=m\,a_y=3000\times11.11=33333\ \mathrm{N}$$
3. Wheelbase $L = 2.1\ \mathrm{m}$ and CG $0.9\ \mathrm{m}$ behind the front axle, so $a = 0.9\ \mathrm{m}$ and $b = 1.2\ \mathrm{m}$.
4. Axle forces:
$$F_f=\frac{F\,b}{L}=\frac{33333\times1.2}{2.1}=19048\ \mathrm{N},\quad F_r=\frac{F\,a}{L}=\frac{33333\times0.9}{2.1}=14286\ \mathrm{N}$$
5. Cornering stiffness $150\ \mathrm{kgf/deg}$ is for the two tyres of an axle combined: $150\times9.81 = 1471.5\ \mathrm{N/deg}$ per axle.
$$C_\alpha=k_{kgf}\,g=150\times9.81=1471.5\ \mathrm{N/deg}$$
6. (i), (ii) Slip angles:
$$\alpha_f=\frac{F_f}{C_\alpha}=\frac{19048}{1471.5}=12.94^\circ,\qquad \alpha_r=\frac{F_r}{C_\alpha}=\frac{14286}{1471.5}=9.71^\circ$$
7. (iii) Since the front slip angle is larger than the rear ($\alpha_f > \alpha_r$), the car understeers.
8. Steering angle needed (small angles, in degrees): Ackermann angle L/R plus the slip-angle difference.
$$\delta=\frac{L}{R}\cdot\frac{180}{\pi}+\alpha_f-\alpha_r=4.81+12.94-9.71=8.04^\circ$$

**Answer:** (i) $\alpha_f = 12.94^\circ$ ; (ii) $\alpha_r = 9.71^\circ$ ($150\ \mathrm{kgf/deg}$ is the combined stiffness of the two tyres of an axle) ; (iii) $\alpha_f > \alpha_r$, so the vehicle understeers.

**Exam tip:** Show lateral force, axle forces, slip angles and the final understeer or oversteer statement with the comparison. State that the stiffness is per axle. The usual slip is using per-tyre stiffness without noting it and so halving or doubling the angle.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
  insert into public.solutions (question_id, version, body_md) values ((select id from public.questions where ext_id = 'm25-3'), 1, '**What it asks:** It wants short definitions of four things: roll steer, steer caused by vertical load, steer caused by lateral load, and bore torque. Give a line and a sketch idea for each.

1. Roll steer: change of toe (steering) of the wheels caused by suspension geometry as the body rolls, for example a semi-trailing arm or a leaf-spring axle swinging in plan. It adds understeer or oversteer without driver input.
2. Steering due to vertical load: with kingpin inclination the wheel end drops as it is steered, so the body is lifted; the weight then produces a moment returning the wheel to straight ahead. Castor also gives a vertical-load moment, but it cancels between left and right wheels when their loads are equal, so it matters only under load transfer.
3. Steering due to lateral load: the side force acts behind the steering axis (castor trail plus pneumatic trail), so the moment $T=F_y\,(t_c+t_p)$ aligns the wheel.
4. Bore torque: torque needed to twist (scrub) the tyre contact patch about the road-normal axis when steering at standstill or very low speed; largest while parking and reduced by power steering. Simple sliding model with a circular patch of radius $R_P$ and uniform pressure:
$$T_{B,max}=\frac{2}{3}\,\mu\,F_z\,R_P$$

**Answer:** Roll steer = geometry-induced toe change on roll; vertical load gives a KPI weight-lift return moment; lateral load gives a castor/pneumatic trail moment; bore torque = contact-patch twisting torque at standstill.

**Exam tip:** Give each of the four items a definition and one cause in two or three lines, with a small sketch where easy. The usual slip is writing only the definition and not the cause.') on conflict (question_id, version) do nothing;
end $seed$;
